E0002: BlankPkgName

This error occurs when a package name is the blank identifier "_".

For example:

    package _

See "Package clause" in the Plutonium spec, section 14.2.

E0003: MismatchedPkgName

This error occurs when a file's package name doesn't match the package name
already established by other files.

See "Package clause" in the Plutonium spec, section 14.2.

E0004: InvalidPkgUse

This error occurs when a package identifier is used outside of a selector
expression.

For example:

    import "fmt"

    var _ = fmt

See "Import declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 14.3.

E0005: BadImportPath

This error occurs when an import path is not valid.

See "Import declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 14.3.

E0006: BrokenImport

The imported package could not be loaded. Check the import path: standard
library packages are under pluto/, and the program's own packages are under the
module path in pluto.mod.

For example:

    import "amissingpackage"

See "Import declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 14.3.

E0007: ImportCRenamed

This error occurs when the special import "C" is renamed. "C" is a
pseudo-package, and must not be renamed.

For example:

    import _ "C"

See "Import declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 14.3.

E0008: UnusedImport

The file imports a package but never uses it. Plutonium, like Go, treats an
unused import as an error, so that a file's imports always show what it really
depends on.

Delete the import, or use the package.

For example:

    import "pluto/fmt"

    func main() {}

Written correctly:

    func main() {}

See "Import declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 14.3.

E0009: InvalidInitCycle

Package-level variables are initialized in an order where each comes after the
ones its initial value uses. Here the values depend on each other in a circle,
so no order works. Break the circle by setting one of them in an init function
or at run time.

For example:

    var x int = f()

    func f() int { return x }

See "Package initialization" in the Plutonium spec, section 15.2.

E0010: DuplicateDecl

The same name is declared twice in one scope. Each name in a block, or at
package level across all the package's files, must be declared once. The message
shows where the other declaration is.

Rename one of them, or remove the duplicate.

For example:

    var x = 1
    var x = 2

See "Declarations and scope" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.

E0011: InvalidDeclCycle

A type, constant or variable is defined in terms of itself, so it has no
well-defined size or value. A struct cannot contain itself directly; it can
contain a pointer to itself.

For example:

    type Node struct {
        next Node
    }

Written correctly:

    type Node struct {
        next *Node
    }

See "Type declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.8.

E0012: InvalidTypeCycle

This error occurs when a cycle in type definitions results in a type that is not
well-defined.

See "Type declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.8.

E0013: InvalidConstInit

A constant's value must be known when compiling: a literal, another constant, or
an expression of those. A variable or function call is only known at run time.
Declare a variable instead.

For example:

    var n = 4
    const size = n * 2

Written correctly:

    const n = 4
    const size = n * 2

See "Constant declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.6.

E0014: InvalidConstVal

This error occurs when a const value cannot be converted to its target type.

For example:

    const _ = 1 << "hello"

See "Constant declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.6.

E0015: InvalidConstType

This error occurs when the underlying type in a const declaration is not a valid
constant type.

For example:

    const c *int = 4

See "Constant declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.6.

E0016: UntypedNilUse

nil has no type of its own: it is the zero value of pointers, slices, interfaces
and func values. A variable declared with := or var and no type takes the type
of its value, and nil gives it none. Write the type.

For example:

    var p = nil

Written correctly:

    var p *Node = nil

See "Variable declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.10.

E0017: WrongAssignCount

The number of values on the right does not match the number of variables on the
left. A call that returns several results must be assigned to that many
variables; use _ for results you do not need.

The functions of pluto/os that return a name or value from the operating system
(DirEntry.Name, Getenv, LookupEnv, Arg, and so on) return an error as well,
because the bytes may not be valid UTF-8, which a string must be. Assign both
results, or use the form with Lossy in its name to display the value, or RawName
and the Bytes forms to get the exact bytes.

For example:

    n := strconv.Atoi(s)

Written correctly:

    n, err := strconv.Atoi(s)

See "Assignment statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.7.

E0018: UnassignableOperand

The left side of an assignment must be something that can hold a value: a
variable, a pointer dereference, a field of an addressable struct, or a slice or
array element. Constants, function results and string bytes cannot be assigned
to.

For example:

    const limit = 10
    limit = 20

See "Assignment statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.7.

E0019: NoNewVar

x := v declares new variables. Every name on the left already exists in this
scope, so nothing would be declared. Use = to assign to the existing variables.

For example:

    x := 1
    x := 2

Written correctly:

    x := 1
    x = 2

See "Short variable declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.11.

E0020: MultiValAssignOp

This error occurs when an assignment operation (+=, *=, etc) does not have
single-valued left-hand or right-hand side.

For example:

    func f() int {
        x, y := 1, 2
        x, y += 1
        return x + y
    }

See "Assignment statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.7.

E0021: InvalidIfaceAssign

A value can be used as an interface only if its type has every method the
interface lists, with the same parameters and results. Types satisfy interfaces
without saying so, so a missing method shows up where the value is used.

The message names the missing or mismatched method. A method declared on *T
belongs to *T, not to T, so you may need to use a pointer.

For example:

    type Shape interface{ Area() float64 }

    type Square struct{ side float64 }

    var s Shape = Square{2}

Written correctly:

    func (q Square) Area() float64 { return q.side * q.side }

    var s Shape = Square{2}

See "Interface types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.9.

E0022: InvalidChanAssign

This error occurs when a chan assignment is invalid.

Per the spec, a value x is assignable to a channel type T if: "x is a
bidirectional channel value, T is a channel type, x's type V and T have
identical element types, and at least one of V or T is not a defined type."

For example:

    type T1 chan int
    type T2 chan int

    var x T1
    // Invalid assignment because both types are named
    var _ T2 = x

See "Assignability" in the Plutonium spec, section 8.4.

E0023: IncompatibleAssign

A value of one type is used where a different type is needed: in an assignment,
a variable declaration, an argument or a return. Plutonium, like Go, never
converts between types by itself, even between int and int64, or between numbers
and text.

Convert explicitly with T(x) between number types, or use a function such as
strconv.Atoi to turn text into a number.

For example:

    var count int = "3"

Written correctly:

    var count int = 3

See "Assignability" in the Plutonium spec, section 8.4.

E0024: UnaddressableFieldAssign

This error occurs when trying to assign to a struct field in a map value.

For example:

    func f() {
        m := make(map[string]struct{i int})
        m["foo"].i = 42
    }

See "Assignment statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.7.

E0025: NotAType

A name used as a type is not a type: it is a variable, constant, function or
package. Check the spelling, and whether a local variable hides a type of the
same name.

For example:

    var size = 4
    var buf size

See "Types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.

E0026: InvalidArrayLen

The length of an array type must be a constant integer that is not negative,
since an array's size is fixed when compiling. For a length known only at run
time, use a vec.Vec from pluto/vec.

For example:

    n := 8
    var buf [n]byte

Written correctly:

    n := 8
    buf := vec.Make[byte](n)
    defer buf.Free()

See "Array types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.4.

E0027: BlankIfaceMethod

This error occurs when a method name is '_'.

For example:

    type T interface {
        _(int)
    }

See "Interface types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.9.

E0028: IncomparableMapKey

This error occurs when a map key type does not support the == and != operators.

For example:

    var x map[T]int

    type T []int

See "Map types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.10.

E0030: InvalidPtrEmbed

This error occurs when an embedded field is of the pointer form *T, and T itself
is itself a pointer, an unsafe.Pointer, or an interface.

For example:

    type T *int

    type S struct {
        *T
    }

See "Struct types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.6.

E0031: BadRecv

This error occurs when a method declaration does not have exactly one receiver
parameter.

For example:

    func () _() {}

See "Method declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.14.

E0032: InvalidRecv

This error occurs when a receiver type expression is not of the form T or *T, or
T is a pointer type.

For example:

    type T struct {}

    func (**T) m() {}

See "Method declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.14.

E0033: DuplicateFieldAndMethod

This error occurs when an identifier appears as both a field and method name.

For example:

    type T struct {
        m int
    }

    func (T) m() {}

See "Method declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.14.

E0034: DuplicateMethod

A type has two methods with the same name. Method names must be unique for each
type, whatever their parameters; there is no overloading.

For example:

    type T struct {}
    func (T) m() {}
    func (T) m(i int) int { return i }

See "Method declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.14.

E0035: InvalidBlank

The blank identifier _ can be assigned to, to discard a value, but cannot be
read. It is not a variable.

For example:

    x := _

See "Blank identifier" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.2.

E0036: InvalidIota

This error occurs when the predeclared identifier iota is used outside of a
constant declaration.

For example:

    var x = iota

See "Iota" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.7.

E0037: MissingInitBody

This error occurs when an init function is missing its body.

For example:

    func init()

See "Package initialization" in the Plutonium spec, section 15.2.

E0038: InvalidInitSig

This error occurs when an init function declares parameters or results.

Deprecated: no longer emitted by the type checker. _InvalidInitDecl is used
instead.

See "Package initialization" in the Plutonium spec, section 15.2.

E0039: InvalidInitDecl

This error occurs when init is declared as anything other than a function.

For example:

    var init = 1

See "Package initialization" in the Plutonium spec, section 15.2.

E0040: InvalidMainDecl

In package main, the name main must be the function where the program starts. It
cannot be a variable or type, and it cannot have type parameters.

See "Program execution" in the Plutonium spec, section 15.4.

E0041: TooManyValues

A function that returns several results is used where one value is expected.
Assign the results to variables first, and use the one you need.

This includes the functions of pluto/os that return a name or value from the
operating system (DirEntry.Name, Getenv, and so on): they return an error as
well, because the bytes may not be valid UTF-8. Use the form with Lossy in its
name to display the value, or RawName and the Bytes forms to get the exact
bytes.

For example:

    x := strconv.Atoi(s) + 1

Written correctly:

    n, err := strconv.Atoi(s)
    x := n + 1

See "Calls" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.14.

E0042: NotAnExpr

Something that is not a value, such as a type or a package name, is used where a
value is needed.

For example:

    type T struct {}

    func f() {
        T
    }

See "Expressions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.

E0043: TruncatedFloat

A constant with a fraction is used as an integer. Converting it would lose the
fraction, which Plutonium, like Go, does not do silently for constants. Use an
integer constant, or convert a variable at run time.

For example:

    var n int = 2.5

See "Representability" in the Plutonium spec, section 8.5.

E0044: NumericOverflow

A constant does not fit in the type it is used as. Each integer type holds a
fixed range: uint8 holds 0 to 255, int8 holds -128 to 127, and so on. Use a
wider type, or a smaller constant.

For example:

    var b uint8 = 256

Written correctly:

    var b uint16 = 256

See "Representability" in the Plutonium spec, section 8.5.

E0045: UndefinedOp

The operator is not defined for this type. For example, + works on numbers and
constant strings, < works on numbers and strings, and ! works only on bools.
Structs, slices and funcs have no arithmetic.

For example:

    var ok bool
    n := ok + 1

See "Operators" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.18.

E0046: MismatchedTypes

The two operands of a binary operator, such as + or ==, must have the same type.
Plutonium, like Go, never converts one to match the other, even between int and
int64.

Convert one operand to the other's type with T(x).

For example:

    var a int = 1
    var b int64 = 2
    c := a + b

Written correctly:

    c := int64(a) + b

See "Arithmetic operators" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.19.

E0047: DivByZero

A constant division by zero. Dividing by a constant 0 is always wrong, so the
compiler reports it rather than let it panic at run time.

For example:

    const divisor = 0
    var x int = 1/divisor

See "Arithmetic operators" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.19.

E0048: NonNumericIncDec

++ and -- work only on numbers.

For example:

    func f() {
        var c = "c"
        c++
    }

See "IncDec statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.6.

E0049: UnaddressableOperand

& takes the address of a variable, a field of an addressable struct, a slice
element or a composite literal. Other values, such as constants and function
results, have no address. Store the value in a variable first.

For example:

    p := &f()

Written correctly:

    v := f()
    p := &v

See "Address operators" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.22.

E0050: InvalidIndirection

* follows a pointer to the value it points at. The operand here is not a
pointer.

For example:

    var x int
    var y = *x

See "Address operators" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.22.

E0051: NonIndexableOperand

Only arrays, slices, strings, pointers to arrays, and generic types whose type
set allows it can be indexed with [i].

For example:

    var x = 1
    var y = x[1]

See "Index expressions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.10.

E0052: InvalidIndex

An index must be an integer that is not negative, and a constant index must be
inside the bounds of an array or constant string.

For example:

    var s = [...]int{1,2,3}
    var x = s[5]

See "Index expressions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.10.

E0053: SwappedSliceIndices

This error occurs when constant indices in a slice expression are decreasing in
value.

For example:

    var _ = []int{1,2,3}[2:1]

See "Slice expressions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.12.

E0054: NonSliceableOperand

This error occurs when a slice operation is applied to a value whose type is not
sliceable, or is unaddressable.

For example:

    var x = [...]int{1, 2, 3}[:1]

See "Slice expressions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.12.

E0055: InvalidSliceExpr

This error occurs when a three-index slice expression (a[x:y:z]) is applied to a
string.

For example:

    var s = "hello"
    var x = s[1:2:3]

See "Slice expressions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.12.

E0056: InvalidShiftCount

The count of a shift, the right operand of << or >>, must be an integer that is
not negative.

For example:

    var (
        x string
        y int = 1 << x
    )

See "Arithmetic operators" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.19.

E0057: InvalidShiftOperand

This error occurs when the shifted operand is not an integer.

For example:

    var s = "hello"
    var x = s << 2

See "Arithmetic operators" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.19.

E0058: InvalidReceive

This error occurs when there is a channel receive from a value that is either
not a channel, or is a send-only channel.

For example:

    func f() {
        var x = 1
        <-x
    }

See "Receive operator" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.23.

E0059: InvalidSend

This error occurs when there is a channel send to a value that is not a channel,
or is a receive-only channel.

For example:

    func f() {
        var x = 1
        x <- "hello!"
    }

See "Send statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.5.

E0060: DuplicateLitKey

A composite literal gives the same index or key twice.

For example:

    var _ = []int{0:1, 0:2}

See "Composite literals" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.3.

E0061: MissingLitKey

This error occurs when a map literal is missing a key expression.

For example:

    var _ = map[string]int{1}

See "Composite literals" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.3.

E0062: InvalidLitIndex

This error occurs when the key in a key-value element of a slice or array
literal is not an integer constant.

For example:

    var i = 0
    var x = []string{i: "world"}

See "Composite literals" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.3.

E0063: OversizeArrayLit

This error occurs when an array literal exceeds its length.

For example:

    var _ = [2]int{1,2,3}

See "Composite literals" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.3.

E0064: MixedStructLit

A struct literal either names every field it sets (Point{X: 1, Y: 2}) or lists a
value for every field in order (Point{1, 2}). It cannot mix the two.

For example:

    p := Point{X: 1, 2}

Written correctly:

    p := Point{X: 1, Y: 2}

See "Composite literals" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.3.

E0065: InvalidStructLit

This error occurs when a positional struct literal has an incorrect number of
values.

For example:

    var _ = struct{i, j int}{1,2,3}

See "Composite literals" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.3.

E0066: MissingLitField

The struct literal names a field that the struct type does not have. Check the
spelling; field names are case-sensitive.

For example:

    var _ = struct{i int}{j: 2}

See "Composite literals" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.3.

E0067: DuplicateLitField

The struct literal sets the same field twice.

For example:

    var _ = struct{i int}{i: 1, i: 2}

See "Composite literals" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.3.

E0068: UnexportedLitField

The literal sets a field of another package's struct whose name starts with a
lower-case letter. Such fields are private to their package. Use a constructor
function from that package, if it has one.

See "Exported identifiers" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.4.

E0069: InvalidLitField

This error occurs when a field name is not a valid identifier.

For example:

    var _ = struct{i int}{1: 1}

See "Composite literals" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.3.

E0070: UntypedLit

This error occurs when a composite literal omits a required type identifier.

For example:

    type outer struct{
        inner struct { i int }
    }

    var _ = outer{inner: {1}}

See "Composite literals" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.3.

E0071: InvalidLit

This error occurs when a composite literal expression does not match its type.

For example:

    type P *struct{
        x int
    }
    var _ = P {}

See "Composite literals" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.3.

E0072: AmbiguousSelector

This error occurs when a selector is ambiguous.

For example:

    type E1 struct { i int }
    type E2 struct { i int }
    type T struct { E1; E2 }

    var x T
    var _ = x.i

See "Selectors" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.7.

E0073: UndeclaredImportedName

The package has no exported name like this. Only names that start with an
upper-case letter can be used from other packages. Check the spelling, and the
package's documentation for the name it uses.

For example:

    import "go/types"

    var _ = types.NotAnActualIdentifier

See "Qualified identifiers" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.2.

E0074: UnexportedName

The name starts with a lower-case letter, so it is private to the package that
declares it and cannot be used from another package.

For example:

    import "reflect"

    type _ reflect.flag

See "Exported identifiers" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.4.

E0075: UndeclaredName

The name is not declared in any scope that reaches this point. It may be
misspelled (names are case-sensitive), declared in a block that has ended, or
belong to a package that is not imported.

For example:

    func main() {
        count := 1
        print(cuont)
    }

See "Declarations and scope" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.

E0076: MissingFieldOrMethod

The type has no field or method with this name. Check the spelling: names are
case-sensitive, and another package's names must start with an upper-case
letter. A method declared on *T can be called on a T variable, but not on a T
value that has no address.

For example:

    type T struct {}

    var x = T{}.f

See "Selectors" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.7.

E0077: BadDotDotDotSyntax

This error occurs when a "..." occurs in a context where it is not valid.

For example:

    var _ = map[int][...]int{0: {}}

See "Passing arguments to ... parameters" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.15.

E0078: NonVariadicDotDotDot

This error occurs when a "..." is used on the final argument to a non-variadic
function.

For example:

    func printArgs(s []string) {
        for _, a := range s {
            println(a)
        }
    }

    func f() {
        s := []string{"a", "b", "c"}
        printArgs(s...)
    }

See "Passing arguments to ... parameters" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.15.

E0081: InvalidDotDotDot

This error occurs when a "..." is used in a non-variadic built-in function.

For example:

    var s = []int{1, 2, 3}
    var l = len(s...)

See "Passing arguments to ... parameters" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.15.

E0082: UncalledBuiltin

A builtin function such as len or cap is used as a value. Builtins can only be
called; wrap the call in a function literal if you need a func value.

For example:

    var _ = copy

See "Built-in functions" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.

E0083: InvalidAppend

append's first argument must be a slice, and the values after it must fit its
element type. Plutonium has no append (a slice cannot grow); use pluto/vec.Vec
for a sequence that grows.

For example:

    var _ = append(1, 2)

See "Built-ins Plutonium does not have" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.1.

E0084: InvalidCap

cap takes an array or a pointer to one, a slice or a channel. Unlike len, it
does not call methods: a library container or a ring reports its capacity with a
method of its own.

Call the type's Cap method instead.

For example:

    println(cap(r))

Written correctly:

    println(r.Cap())

See "Length and capacity" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.6.

E0085: InvalidClose

This error occurs when close(...) is called with an argument that is not of
channel type, or that is a receive-only channel.

For example:

    func f() {
        var x int
        close(x)
    }

See "Close" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.4.

E0086: InvalidCopy

This error occurs when the arguments are not of slice type or do not have
compatible type.

See https://golang.org/ref/spec#Appending_and_copying_slices for more
information on the type requirements for the copy built-in.

For example:

    func f() {
        var x []int
        y := []int64{1,2,3}
        copy(x, y)
    }

See "Copying slices" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.2.

E0087: InvalidComplex

This error occurs when the complex built-in function is called with arguments
with incompatible types.

For example:

    var _ = complex(float32(1), float64(2))

See "Built-ins Plutonium does not have" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.1.

E0088: InvalidDelete

This error occurs when the delete built-in function is called with a first
argument that is not a map.

For example:

    func f() {
        m := "hello"
        delete(m, "e")
    }

See "Deletion of map elements" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.5.

E0089: InvalidImag

This error occurs when the imag built-in function is called with an argument
that does not have complex type.

For example:

    var _ = imag(int(1))

See "Built-ins Plutonium does not have" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.1.

E0090: InvalidLen

len takes a string, an array or a pointer to one, a slice, a map, a channel, an
owned, shared or synced array, or a value whose type has a method Len() int,
such as a library container or a ring; len(E) of an enum type E is its number of
members. A Len method with a pointer receiver needs an operand that is
addressable or a pointer, as a method call would.

Pass a value of one of these types, or store the value in a variable first.

For example:

    println(len(newRing()))

Written correctly:

    r := newRing()
    println(len(r))

See "Length and capacity" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.6.

E0091: SwappedMakeArgs

This error occurs when make is called with three arguments, and its length
argument is larger than its capacity argument.

For example:

    var x = make([]int, 3, 2)

See "Making channels and maps" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.7.

E0092: InvalidMake

This error occurs when make is called with an unsupported type argument.

See https://golang.org/ref/spec#Making_slices_maps_and_channels for information
on the types that may be created using make.

For example:

    var x = make(int)

See "Making channels and maps" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.7.

E0093: InvalidReal

This error occurs when the real built-in function is called with an argument
that does not have complex type.

For example:

    var _ = real(int(1))

See "Built-ins Plutonium does not have" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.1.

E0094: InvalidAssert

A type assertion x.(T) asks which concrete type an interface value holds, so x
must be an interface. This value is not an interface; its type is already known.

For example:

    var x = 1
    var _ = x.(float64)

See "Type assertions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.13.

E0095: ImpossibleAssert

The type in the assertion does not have every method of the interface, so no
value of the interface can hold it, and the assertion would always fail.

For example:

    type T int

    func (t *T) m() int { return int(*t) }

    type I interface { m() int }

    var x I
    var _ = x.(T)

See "Type assertions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.13.

E0096: InvalidConversion

This conversion is not allowed. T(x) converts between number types, between
types with the same underlying type, and between pointers and unsafe.Pointer. It
does not turn numbers into text: string(65) would make a rune, not "65".

For example:

    var x float64
    var _ = string(x)

See "Conversions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.24.

E0097: InvalidUntypedConversion

This error occurs when there is no valid implicit conversion from an untyped
value satisfying the type constraints of the context in which it is used.

For example:

    func f[T ~int8 | ~int16 | ~int32 | ~int64](x T) T {
        return x + 1024
    }

See "Conversions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.24.

E0098: BadOffsetofSyntax

This error occurs when unsafe.Offsetof is called with an argument that is not a
selector expression.

For example:

    import "unsafe"

    var x int
    var _ = unsafe.Offsetof(x)

See "Package unsafe" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.1.

E0099: InvalidOffsetof

This error occurs when unsafe.Offsetof is called with a method selector, rather
than a field selector, or when the field is embedded via a pointer.

"If f is an embedded field, it must be reachable without pointer indirections
through fields of the struct. "

For example:

    import "unsafe"

    type T struct { f int }
    type S struct { *T }
    var s S
    var _ = unsafe.Offsetof(s.f)

See "Package unsafe" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.1.

E0100: UnusedExpr

The expression computes a value and throws it away, so it has no effect. A
statement must do something: call a function, assign, send, and so on. Remove
it, or assign the value to _ if computing it is the point.

For example:

    x + 1

Written correctly:

    x = x + 1

See "Expression statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.4.

E0101: UnusedVar

A local variable is declared but its value is never used. Plutonium, like Go,
treats this as an error, because it often means a mistake, such as assigning to
the wrong variable.

Remove the variable, use it, or assign it to _ for now.

For example:

    func main() {
        n := compute()
    }

Written correctly:

    func main() {
        _ = compute()
    }

See "Variable declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.10.

E0102: MissingReturn

A function that declares results must end every path through its body with a
return statement (or a panic, or an endless loop). The compiler found a path
that reaches the closing brace without one.

Add a return at the end, or a panic if that point can never be reached.

For example:

    func sign(x int) int {
        if x < 0 {
            return -1
        }
    }

Written correctly:

    func sign(x int) int {
        if x < 0 {
            return -1
        }
        return 1
    }

See "Terminating statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.1.

E0103: WrongResultCount

The return statement gives a different number of values than the function
declares as results. Return one value for each result, in order.

For example:

    func f() (int, error) {
        return 0
    }

Written correctly:

    func f() (int, error) {
        return 0, nil
    }

See "Return statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.14.

E0104: OutOfScopeResult

This error occurs when the name of a value implicitly returned by an empty
return statement is shadowed in a nested scope.

For example:

    func factor(n int) (i int) {
        for i := 2; i < n; i++ {
            if n%i == 0 {
                return
            }
        }
        return 0
    }

See "Return statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.14.

E0105: InvalidCond

The condition of an if or for statement must be a bool. Plutonium, like Go, does
not treat numbers or pointers as true or false; compare explicitly.

For example:

    if n {
    }

Written correctly:

    if n != 0 {
    }

See "If statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.8.

E0106: InvalidPostDecl

This error occurs when there is a declaration in a for-loop post statement.

For example:

    func f() {
        for i := 0; i < 10; j := 0 {}
    }

See "For statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.10.

E0108: InvalidIterVar

This error occurs when two iteration variables are used while ranging over a
channel.

For example:

    func f(c chan int) {
        for k, v := range c {
            println(k, v)
        }
    }

See "For statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.10.

E0109: InvalidRangeExpr

for range works over arrays, slices, strings, integers, channels, enum types and
iterator functions. This value is none of those.

For example:

    func f(f float64) {
        for j := range f {
            println(j)
        }
    }

See "For statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.10.

E0110: MisplacedBreak

break must be inside a for, switch or select statement.

For example:

    func f() {
        break
    }

See "Break statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.15.

E0111: MisplacedContinue

continue must be inside a for statement.

For example:

    func sumeven(n int) int {
        proceed := func() {
            continue
        }
        sum := 0
        for i := 1; i <= n; i++ {
            if i % 2 != 0 {
                proceed()
            }
            sum += i
        }
        return sum
    }

See "Continue statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.16.

E0112: MisplacedFallthrough

This error occurs when a fallthrough statement is not within an expression
switch.

For example:

    func typename(i interface{}) string {
        switch i.(type) {
        case int64:
            fallthrough
        case int:
            return "int"
        }
        return "unsupported"
    }

See "Fallthrough statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.18.

E0113: DuplicateCase

A switch lists the same constant value in two cases. Only the first could ever
match.

For example:

    func printInt(i int) {
        switch i {
        case 1:
            println("one")
        case 1:
            println("One")
        }
    }

See "Switch statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.9.

E0114: DuplicateDefault

This error occurs when a type or expression switch has multiple default clauses.

For example:

    func printInt(i int) {
        switch i {
        case 1:
            println("one")
        default:
            println("One")
        default:
            println("1")
        }
    }

See "Switch statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.9.

E0115: BadTypeKeyword

This error occurs when a .(type) expression is used anywhere other than a type
switch.

For example:

    type I interface {
        m()
    }
    var t I
    var _ = t.(type)

See "Switch statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.9.

E0116: InvalidTypeSwitch

This error occurs when .(type) is used on an expression that is not of interface
type.

For example:

    func f(i int) {
        switch x := i.(type) {}
    }

See "Switch statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.9.

E0117: InvalidExprSwitch

A case value cannot be compared with the switch value: their types differ, or
the type cannot be compared with ==.

For example:

    func _() {
        var a struct{ _ func() }
        switch a /* ERROR cannot switch on a */ {
        }
    }

See "Switch statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.9.

E0118: InvalidSelectCase

This error occurs when a select case is not a channel send or receive.

For example:

    func checkChan(c <-chan int) bool {
        select {
        case c:
            return true
        default:
            return false
        }
    }

See "Select statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.13.

E0119: UndeclaredLabel

This error occurs when an undeclared label is jumped to.

For example:

    func f() {
        goto L
    }

See "Label scopes" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.1.

E0120: DuplicateLabel

This error occurs when a label is declared more than once.

For example:

    func f() int {
    L:
    L:
        return 1
    }

See "Label scopes" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.1.

E0121: MisplacedLabel

This error occurs when a break or continue label is not on a for, switch, or
select statement.

For example:

    func f() {
    L:
        a := []int{1,2,3}
        for _, e := range a {
            if e > 10 {
                break L
            }
            println(a)
        }
    }

See "Labeled statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.3.

E0122: UnusedLabel

A label is declared but no break, continue or goto uses it. Remove it.

For example:

    func f() {
    L:
    }

See "Labeled statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.3.

E0123: JumpOverDecl

This error occurs when a label jumps over a variable declaration.

For example:

    func f() int {
        goto L
        x := 2
    L:
        x++
        return x
    }

See "Goto statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.17.

E0124: JumpIntoBlock

This error occurs when a forward jump goes to a label inside a nested block.

For example:

    func f(x int) {
        goto L
        if x > 0 {
        L:
            print("inside block")
        }
    }

See "Goto statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.17.

E0125: InvalidMethodExpr

This error occurs when a pointer method is called but the argument is not
addressable.

For example:

    type T struct {}

    func (*T) m() int { return 1 }

    var _ = T.m(T{})

See "Method expressions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.8.

E0126: WrongArgCount

The call passes a different number of arguments than the function has
parameters. The message shows the arguments given (have) and the parameters
(want). Parameters with default values may be left out from the end.

For example:

    func add(a, b int) int { return a + b }

    x := add(1, 2, 3)

Written correctly:

    x := add(1, 2)

See "Calls" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.14.

E0127: InvalidCall

Only functions, methods and func values can be called. The expression before the
parentheses is something else, or a type used in a way that is not a conversion.

For example:

    var x = "x"
    var y = x()

See "Calls" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.14.

E0128: UnusedResults

The result of this call must be used. Builtins such as len and cap, and
conversions, have no effect other than their result.

For example:

    func f(a []int) int {
        defer len(a)
        return i
    }

See "Expression statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.4.

E0129: InvalidDefer

This error occurs when a deferred expression is not a function call, for example
if the expression is a type conversion.

For example:

    func f(i int) int {
        defer int32(i)
        return i
    }

See "Defer statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.19.

E0130: InvalidGo

This error occurs when a go expression is not a function call, for example if
the expression is a type conversion.

For example:

    func f(i int) int {
        go int32(i)
        return i
    }

See "Task statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.12.

E0131: BadDecl

This error occurs when a declaration has invalid syntax.

See "Declarations and scope" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.

E0132: RepeatedDecl

This error occurs when an identifier occurs more than once on the left hand side
of a short variable declaration.

For example:

    func _() {
        x, y, y := 1, 2, 3
    }

See "Declarations and scope" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.

E0133: InvalidUnsafeAdd

This error occurs when unsafe.Add is called with a length argument that is not
of integer type. It also occurs if it is used in a package compiled for a
language version before go1.17.

For example:

    import "unsafe"

    var p unsafe.Pointer
    var _ = unsafe.Add(p, float64(1))

See "Package unsafe" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.1.

E0134: InvalidUnsafeSlice

This error occurs when unsafe.Slice is called with a pointer argument that is
not of pointer type or a length argument that is not of integer type, negative,
or out of bounds. It also occurs if it is used in a package compiled for a
language version before go1.17.

For example:

    import "unsafe"

    var x int
    var _ = unsafe.Slice(x, 1)

See "Package unsafe" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.1.

E0135: UnsupportedFeature

This feature is not available in the language version being compiled.

See "Language version" in the Plutonium spec, section 21.8.

E0136: NotAGenericType

Type arguments in square brackets are given to a type that has no type
parameters.

For example:

    type T int

    var _ T[int]

See "Instantiations" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.16.

E0137: WrongTypeArgCount

A generic function or type gets a different number of type arguments than it has
type parameters. Give one for each, or leave out the trailing ones that can be
inferred from the arguments.

For example:

    type T[p any] int

    var _ T[int, string]

See "Instantiations" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.16.

E0138: CannotInferTypeArgs

A generic function was called without type arguments, and the compiler could not
work them out from the call's arguments. A type parameter that appears only in
the results, or only in parameters that were left out, cannot be inferred. Give
the type arguments explicitly in square brackets.

For example:

    func Zero[T any]() T { var z T; return z }

    x := Zero()

Written correctly:

    x := Zero[int]()

See "Type inference" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.17.

E0139: InvalidTypeArg

A type argument does not satisfy its type parameter's constraint. The constraint
is an interface that lists the methods the type needs, or the set of types
allowed (such as ~int | ~float64). The message says which part the type fails.

For example:

    type T[P ~int] struct{}

    var _ T[string]

See "Type parameter declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.9.

E0140: InvalidInstanceCycle

This error occurs when an invalid cycle is detected within the instantiation
graph.

For example:

    func f[T any]() { f[*T]() }

See "Instantiations" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.16.

E0141: InvalidUnion

This error occurs when an embedded union or approximation element is not valid.

For example:

    type _ interface {
         ~int | interface{ m() }
    }

See "Interface types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.9.

E0142: MisplacedConstraintIface

This error occurs when a constraint-type interface is used outside of constraint
position.

For example:

     type I interface { ~int }

     var _ I

See "Interface types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.9.

E0143: InvalidMethodTypeParams

This error occurs when methods have type parameters.

It cannot be encountered with an AST parsed using go/parser.

See "Methods with type parameters" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.15.

E0144: MisplacedTypeParam

This error occurs when a type parameter is used in a place where it is not
permitted.

For example:

    type T[P any] P

See "Type parameter declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.9.

E0145: InvalidUnsafeSliceData

This error occurs when unsafe.SliceData is called with an argument that is not
of slice type. It also occurs if it is used in a package compiled for a language
version before go1.20.

For example:

    import "unsafe"

    var x int
    var _ = unsafe.SliceData(x)

See "Package unsafe" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.1.

E0146: InvalidUnsafeString

This error occurs when unsafe.String is called with a length argument that is
not of integer type, negative, or out of bounds. It also occurs if it is used in
a package compiled for a language version before go1.20.

For example:

    import "unsafe"

    var b [10]byte
    var _ = unsafe.String(&b[0], -1)

See "Package unsafe" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.1.

E0148: InvalidClear

This error occurs when clear is called with an argument that is not of map or
slice type.

For example:

    func _(x int) {
        clear(x)
    }

See "Clear" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.3.

E0149: TypeTooLarge

This error occurs if unsafe.Sizeof or unsafe.Offsetof is called with an
expression whose type is too large.

For example:

    import "unsafe"

    type E [1 << 31 - 1]int
    var a [1 << 31]E
    var _ = unsafe.Sizeof(a)

See "Implementation limits" in the Plutonium spec, section 21.4.

E0150: InvalidMinMaxOperand

This error occurs if min or max is called with an operand that cannot be ordered
because it does not support the < operator.

For example:

    const _ = min(true)

See "Min and max" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.8.

E0151: TooNew

This error indicates that, through build tags or a go.mod file, a source file
requires a version of Go that is newer than the logic of the type checker. As a
consequence, the type checker may produce spurious errors or fail to report real
errors. The solution is to rebuild the application with a newer Go release.

See "Language version" in the Plutonium spec, section 21.8.

E1000: SyntaxError

The parser could not read this part of the file as Plutonium. The message says
what it expected and what it found instead. The mistake is often just before the
position shown, such as a missing bracket or operator on the line before.

See "Grammar" in the Plutonium spec, section E.

E1001: UnexpectedToken

The parser expected one thing here and found another. The mistake is often just
before the position shown: a missing comma, bracket or operator, or a keyword
used as a name.

For example:

    f(a b)

Written correctly:

    f(a, b)

See "Grammar" in the Plutonium spec, section E.

E1002: UnclosedBlock

The file ended while a block was still open: a { has no matching }. The parser
reports the error at the end of the file, but the missing } belongs somewhere
earlier.

For example:

    func main() {
        if ok {
            run()
    }

Written correctly:

    func main() {
        if ok {
            run()
        }
    }

See "Blocks" in the Plutonium spec, section 9.

E1003: StmtOutsideFunc

Outside a function, a file may contain only declarations: package, import,
const, var, type and func. Statements such as assignments, calls and if or for
statements must be inside a function body.

The short form x := value is a statement too, so at package level write var x =
value.

For example:

    count := 1

    func main() {}

Written correctly:

    var count = 1

    func main() {}

See "Short variable declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.11.

E1004: MissingSemicolon

Statements end at the end of a line, or at a semicolon. The parser found the end
of one statement and then more text on the same line that does not start a new
one. This usually means a missing operator, comma or bracket, or two statements
on one line.

For example:

    x := a b

Written correctly:

    x := a + b

See "Semicolons" in the Plutonium spec, section 4.3.

E1005: MissingOperand

The parser expected a value here, such as a name, a number or a call, and found
an operator or keyword instead. An operand is missing, or there is an extra
operator.

For example:

    x := := 1

Written correctly:

    x := 1

See "Operands" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.1.

E1006: BraceOnNextLine

The { that opens a block must be on the same line as the if, for, switch or func
that it belongs to. A line break ends the statement there, so a { on the next
line is read as a separate block.

For example:

    if ok
    {
        run()
    }

Written correctly:

    if ok {
        run()
    }

See "Semicolons" in the Plutonium spec, section 4.3.

E1007: BadImportDecl

An import names a package with a string in double quotes, and all imports come
right after the package clause, before any other declaration.

For example:

    import pluto/fmt

Written correctly:

    import "pluto/fmt"

See "Import declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 14.3.

E1008: BadLiteral

A string, rune or number is not written correctly, or the file contains a
character that is not allowed outside strings and comments. Strings in double
quotes must end on the same line; use a raw string in backquotes for text over
several lines. Rune literals in single quotes hold exactly one character. Curly
quotation marks, often pasted from a word processor, are not quotes to the
compiler.

For example:

    s := "unterminated
    t := 'ab'

Written correctly:

    s := "terminated"
    t := "ab"

See "Lexical elements" in the Plutonium spec, section 4.

E1009: MissingPackage

Every source file starts with a package clause, such as package main, before
anything else except comments. All files in one directory belong to the same
package.

Written correctly:

    package main

    func main() {}

See "Package clause" in the Plutonium spec, section 14.2.

E1011: IfaceMethodTParams

Methods may have type parameters of their own, but an interface cannot declare
such a method. A call through an interface does not know which type's method it
reaches, so it could not pick an instance of a generic method.

See "Methods with type parameters" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.15.

E1012: GoStatement

A task is started with the task statement, written like a call: task
worker(jobs). The word go is reserved so that a statement that starts with it
gets this message, but it starts nothing in Plutonium.

Plutonium has no goroutines. A task runs on its own fixed-size stack and is
scheduled by the runtime library, so the statement that starts one has its own
name.

For example:

    go worker(jobs)

Written correctly:

    task worker(jobs)

See "Task statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.12.

E1013: OrOperand

An or clause tests the error that a call returns, so or must come right after a
call: a function, method or function value with its arguments. Here something
else is in front of or: an operator, parentheses, a selector, or a list of
values. Such an operand could hide which call's error the clause tests.

For example:

    total := strconv.Atoi(s) + 1 or return

Written correctly:

    n := strconv.Atoi(s) or return
    total := n + 1

See "Or clauses" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.27.

E2004: RecoverNotPermitted

In Plutonium, panic stops the program. A hosted program first runs the pending
deferred calls, as Go does, but control never returns to the function that
panicked, so there is nothing for recover to catch.

Report failures that the caller can handle by returning an error instead.

Resuming a function after a panic needs an unwinder, tables and runtime support
that a kernel cannot rely on, and a panic in kernel code means the state is
already wrong.

For example:

    defer func() {
        if r := recover(); r != nil {
            log(r)
        }
    }()

Return an error instead:

    func parse(s string) (int, error) {
        if s == "" {
            return 0, errors.New("empty input")
        }
        ...
    }

See "Built-ins Plutonium does not have" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.1.

E2005: ReflectNotPermitted

Plutonium has no reflect package. Reflection needs a description of every type
in the running program.

Use a type switch, or generics, to handle values of several types.

Plutonium keeps type descriptors only for the types a program converts to an
interface, so binaries stay small enough for a kernel.

For example:

    import "reflect"

Written correctly:

    switch v := x.(type) {
    case int:
        ...
    case string:
        ...
    }

See "Import declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 14.3.

E2006: ComplexNotPermitted

Plutonium has no complex number types (complex64 and complex128) and no complex,
real or imag builtins.

Use a struct of two floats for the real and imaginary parts.

Complex numbers are rarely useful in systems code, and leaving them out keeps
the code generator small.

For example:

    var z complex128 = 1 + 2i

Use a struct:

    type Complex struct{ Re, Im float64 }

See "Numeric types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.2.

E2007: MissingAllocatorType

new and make take an optional allocator as their last argument. Its type,
Allocator, is declared in package pluto/rt, which this build could not find.
This happens only when the standard library is missing or incomplete.

Check that PLUTOROOT, if set, points at a directory that contains pluto/rt.

See "Allocation" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.9.

E2008: ErrorsNewNotConstant

errors.New takes only a constant message, such as a string literal. The compiler
makes an error type for each place errors.New is called, with a counter in the
program image, so the message must be known when compiling.

To build a message from values at run time, use fmt.Errorf, which allocates the
error.

In Go, errors.New allocates a new error on every call. Plutonium never allocates
behind the program's back, so each evaluation of errors.New is a new error made
from the call site's type and a counter, which costs one atomic addition and
needs no allocator.

For example:

    return errors.New("open " + name)

Written correctly:

    return fmt.Errorf("open %s", name)

See "Sentinels and errors.New" in the Plutonium spec, section 16.3.

E2009: BadMainSignature

func main takes no arguments. It may return nothing, as in Go, or one int64,
which becomes the program's exit status.

Read command-line arguments with pluto/os instead of parameters.

Either form works:

    func main() { ... }
    func main() int64 { return 0 }

See "Function declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.12.

E2010: NotSendable

A value that crosses tasks, as the argument or receiver of a task statement or
as the element of a channel, must be sendable. This type holds a shared[T],
whose reference count is changed with ordinary arithmetic, so two tasks copying
or dropping references at once would corrupt it. That holds through pointers,
slices, maps and interfaces too: two tasks that reach one struct through a
pointer could both copy the shared[T] out of it. Starting a method of a shared
box with a task statement (task z.Serve()) hands the box itself to the new task,
so it needs a synced box as well.

Use synced[T], which counts atomically.

Go's garbage collector makes every pointer safe to share. Plutonium counts
shared references without atomic instructions, which is only correct on one
task.

For example:

    type Job struct {
        data shared[Buffer]
    }

    jobs := make(chan Job, 16)

Count the references atomically:

    type Job struct {
        data synced[Buffer]
    }

    jobs := make(chan Job, 16)

See "Sendable types" in the Plutonium spec, section 8.8.

E2011: FrameViewSent

This value points into the current function's stack frame: the address of a
local variable or of part of one, a slice of a local array, or the address of a
composite literal. A task statement or a channel send hands it to another task,
which may still use it after this function has returned and its frame is gone.

Start the work with a sync.Scope instead: it waits for the tasks it starts
before its block ends, so they may use the function's variables. Or allocate the
variable with new, so that it outlives the frame, or make it a package-level
variable.

Go moves such variables to the heap without saying so. Plutonium never allocates
behind the program's back, so a local stays in its frame.

For example:

    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    wg.Add(1)
    task worker(&wg)

Start the work with a sync.Scope, which waits for it before its block ends:

    var s sync.Scope
    s.Task(func() { worker(&state) })

See "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3.

E2012: InvalidUTF8String

Every string in Plutonium holds valid UTF-8 text, so a string literal whose
escapes spell out bytes that are not valid UTF-8 is an error. Here the escapes
make a byte or a sequence of bytes that does not encode a character.

Binary data belongs in a []byte or a byte array, which may hold any bytes. To
write the character itself, use its \u escape or type it.

Go strings may hold any bytes. Plutonium guarantees that a string is text, so
that ranging over it, slicing it between runes and the library's rune functions
never meet broken encodings, and so that ASCII strings can be recognized once
and taken fast paths for.

For example:

    const magic = "\x7fELF\xff"

Written correctly:

    var magic = [4]byte{0x7f, 'E', 'L', 0xff}

See "String literals" in the Plutonium spec, section 4.11.

E2013: AppendNotPermitted

Plutonium has no append. In Go, append grows a slice by allocating a larger
array behind the program's back, and the garbage collector frees the old one. A
Plutonium slice is a view of an array that something else owns, so it cannot
grow.

Use a vec.Vec from pluto/vec, which owns its array and grows it from an
allocator. To fill a slice's spare capacity without growing it, slice it longer
and assign, or use copy.

Plutonium has no garbage collector and never allocates memory unless the program
asks for it in the code, so that every cost is visible and the same code can run
inside a kernel.

For example:

    var s []int
    s = append(s, 1)
    s = append(s, 2, 3)

Written correctly:

    s := vec.New[int]()
    s.Push(1)
    s.Append(2, 3)

See "Built-ins Plutonium does not have" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.1.

E2014: MakeSliceNotPermitted

Plutonium has no make for slices. In Go, make([]T, n) allocates an array that
the garbage collector frees when no slice refers to it. A Plutonium slice is a
view of an array that something else owns, so a new array needs an owner to free
it.

vec.Make[T](n) from pluto/vec returns a vec.Vec[T] of n zero elements, which
owns its array and frees it in Free; vec.WithCapacity[T](c) is make([]T, 0, c).
v.Slice() lends the elements as a slice, and v[i] and len(v) work on the Vec
itself. For a size known when the program is compiled, an array needs no
allocation at all.

Plutonium has no garbage collector and never allocates memory unless the program
asks for it in the code, so that every cost is visible and the same code can run
inside a kernel.

For example:

    buf := make([]byte, n)
    read(buf)

Written correctly:

    buf := vec.Make[byte](n)
    defer buf.Free()
    read(buf.Slice())

See "Making channels and maps" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.7.

E2015: BytesToStringNotPermitted

Plutonium has no string(b) for a byte slice b. Every string holds valid UTF-8
text, but a slice of bytes can hold anything. In Go the conversion keeps the
bytes. Here it would have to change each byte that is not valid UTF-8 into the
replacement character U+FFFD without telling you, so two different slices, such
as {0xfe} and {0xff}, would give equal strings.

Say what you mean. strings.FromUTF8(b) returns the string and an error, and
fails if the bytes are not valid UTF-8. strings.FromUTF8Lossy(b) returns a
string in which each invalid byte is replaced by U+FFFD. If the bytes are binary
data, such as a digest used as a map key, keep them in a byte array or a slice
and not in a string.

Go strings may hold any bytes. Plutonium strings are text, so a conversion from
bytes must either check them or replace some of them, and it must not do the
second without the program saying so. The data in a string must not change
silently.

For example:

    s := string(b)

Written correctly:

    s, err := strings.FromUTF8(b)

See "Conversions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.24.

E2016: StringToSliceNotPermitted

Plutonium has no []byte(s) or []rune(s) for a string s. In Go the conversion
allocates a new slice and the garbage collector frees it later. A Plutonium
slice is a view of an array that something else owns, so a copy of a string
needs an owner to free it.

strings.Bytes(s) and strings.Runes(s) from pluto/strings return a vec.Vec that
owns the copy and frees it when it is dropped; v.Slice() lends the elements as a
slice. To fill a buffer of your own without allocating, use copy(buf[:], s),
which works from a string into a []byte. To visit the runes of a string, range
over it, and to count them, use utf8.RuneCountInString(s).

Go allocates the copy behind the program's back and leaves the garbage collector
to free it. Plutonium never allocates unless the code says so, and every
allocation has an owner, so that every cost is visible and the same code can run
inside a kernel.

For example:

    b := []byte(s)
    r := []rune(s)

Written correctly:

    b := strings.Bytes(s)
    r := strings.Runes(s)

See "Conversions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.24.

E2017: IntToStringNotPermitted

string(i) of an integer does not give the digits of i. In Go it gives the
character whose code is i, so string(65) is "A" and string(n) for n := 65 is "A"
too, not "65". Go's own vet tool reports it because programmers expect the
digits.

Plutonium accepts a conversion to string from a value of type rune and from an
untyped constant such as string('A'), and nothing else, so the choice is visible
in the source. A defined type is not accepted even when it is defined as rune or
int32 (type Fd int32), because string(Fd(3)) cannot be told from a conversion
meant to write the number. To make the character, convert to a rune first:
string(rune(i)). To make the digits, use strconv.Itoa(i), or strconv.FormatInt
and strconv.FormatUint for other integer types.

The same holds for a slice: string(s) accepts a slice whose element type is
rune, whatever the name of the slice type, and not a slice of myRune or Fd.
Write each element with rune(c) to a strings.Builder.

A conversion that reads like formatting but means something else is a trap that
Go itself warns about. Plutonium has no false friends: the program says which of
the two it means.

For example:

    n := 65
    s := string(n)

Written correctly:

    n := 65
    s := strconv.Itoa(n)

See "Conversions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.24.

E2018: NotPortable

A type parameter declared [T portable] stands for a value that can be sent
between address spaces, such as two operating system processes that share no
memory. Its value must mean the same to the receiver as to the sender, so it may
refer to nothing in the sender's memory. A portable type is made only of
numbers, booleans, enums and packed types, and of arrays, structs, unions, ring,
volatile and aligned types of those. A pointer, slice, string, function value,
channel, map, interface value, box (owned, shared, synced, weak) or owner
anywhere inside the type makes it not portable. An owner is not portable even
when it is built from numbers, because sending it would also transfer the duty
to drop it.

The message names the first field that stops the type and says why. Replace it
with data that holds its own contents, such as a fixed-size array of bytes and a
length in place of a string.

Go has one address space, so any value can be passed anywhere and a string or
pointer always means the same thing. A Plutonium kernel and its processes have
separate address spaces, and the compiler checks what may cross instead of
leaving the receiver to read a stale address.

For example:

    type Header struct {
        name string
    }

    func Send[T portable](port int, v T) {}

    Send(1, Header{})

Written correctly:

    type Header struct {
        name  [16]byte
        nameN uint8
    }

See "Portable types" in the Plutonium spec, section 8.9.

E2019: StringSliceNotPermitted

A string cannot be cut with a slice expression such as s[i:j]. Every string
holds valid UTF-8 text, and a character can take up to four bytes, so a cut at
an arbitrary byte offset could split one in two. In Go the cut works and leaves
a string that is not valid text. Here the cut would have to panic for some
offsets, so s[:40] would work for English text and panic on the first accented
letter.

Say that the offsets are positions between characters. strings.Slice(s, i, j)
and strings.SliceFrom(s, i) take the substring and panic unless each bound is on
a character boundary (0, len(s), or the offset of a byte that is not a
continuation byte). The substring shares the memory of s. An offset from
strings.Index and the other search functions is always on a boundary. To keep at
most n bytes, use strings.Truncate(s, n), which rounds down to a boundary, and
utf8.RuneStartBefore(s, i) to find the boundary yourself. Arrays and slices are
still cut with a[i:j], and s[i] reads one byte.

Go lets a program cut a string anywhere and does not check that the pieces are
text. A Plutonium string is always valid UTF-8, and code that is valid Go must
not compile and then fail on the first non-ASCII input. The cut has a name, so
the check is visible in the source, and the compiler turns the call into the
same code as a slice expression.

For example:

    head := title[:40]

Written correctly:

    head := strings.Truncate(title, 40)

See "Slice expressions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.12.

E2020: UnsendableToError

Every error value may be sent to another task, so a value can become an error
(or an interface that embeds error) only if its type is sendable. This type
holds a shared[T], whose reference count is changed with ordinary arithmetic, so
two tasks copying or dropping references to it at once would corrupt it.

The message names the shared[T]. alloc.Synced allocates an object whose
references are counted atomically.

Go's garbage collector makes every error safe to share. Plutonium counts a
shared reference without atomic instructions, which is only correct on one task,
and it counts the references to an error object in the object, atomically.

For example:

    type Failure struct {
        detail shared[Detail]
    }

    func (f Failure) Error() string { return "failed" }

    func fail(f Failure) error {
        return f
    }

Written correctly:

    type Failure struct {
        detail synced[Detail]
    }

    func (f Failure) Error() string { return "failed" }

    func fail(f Failure) error {
        return f
    }

See "Sendable types" in the Plutonium spec, section 8.8.

E2021: BadPackedBytePlacement

A big-endian packed type with a byte array, a []byte or no backing numbers its
bits byte by byte, and stores a field of several bytes with its most significant
byte first. A bit field may not cross a byte boundary, since there is no single
order for its bits. A field of whole bytes (a uint16, say) must start on a byte
boundary.

Put the field in a packed type of its own with an integer backing, and use that
type as a field. The nested type is then laid out as an integer, whatever the
bytes around it.

For example:

    type Header packed be [4]byte {
        version bits[uint8, 4]
        offset  bits[uint16, 13]
        flags   bits[uint8, 3]
    }

Written correctly:

    type Frag packed be uint16 {
        offset bits[uint16, 13]
        flags  bits[uint8, 3]
    }

    type Header packed be [4]byte {
        version bits[uint8, 4]
        _       bits[uint8, 4]
        frag    Frag
        _       uint8
    }

See "packed types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.14.

E2022: PerCPUAccess

A //pu:percpu variable has one copy for each processor. A lone load or a lone
store of the whole variable, when it is no larger than a machine word, is one
instruction and is allowed anywhere, as long as the store does not depend on an
earlier load of the same variable. Every other use must be inside a region where
the task cannot move to another processor: a compound assignment or ++, a read
and a write of the variable in one statement, a variable larger than a word or a
part of one, a call of a method with a pointer receiver, taking its address, and
a store whose value, or whose controlling condition, depends on an earlier load
of the same variable.

That last case is a read-modify-write written as two statements: x := ticks
followed by ticks = x + 1, or if current == idle { current = next }. The
dependence is followed through local variables, copies of structs and arrays,
arithmetic, conditions, loops and calls: the result of a call depends on its
arguments and on what the called functions load, and a function that stores the
variable from its argument, or in a branch the caller's condition controls,
counts as a store at the call. The analysis is conservative where it cannot see:
a store of the variable after a load of it is rejected unless the value and
every condition that controls the store are shown not to depend on the load. A
call that could carry the loaded value (a pointer, slice, map, channel or func
argument, a func value, a method value, a function without a body, a defer or
task statement) is assumed to carry it, so are the stores through a pointer from
&v and the stores to parts of the variable, and a value kept in another per-CPU
variable. The message says where the analysis cannot see. The load and the store
must be inside the same region, not in two regions one after the other, and a
task started inside a region is not covered by it. A call that may switch tasks
(rt.Yield, rt.Preempt, a channel operation or another wait) ends a region in
which the task cannot move, since the task may resume on another processor.
Outside such a region the task could move in the middle of the use, and read one
processor's copy and write another's.

A region is code between rt.DisablePreemption and rt.EnablePreemption, between
x86.Cli and x86.Sti or x86.SaveFlagsCli and x86.RestoreFlags, or with an rt.Lock
held, on every path to the use, including a use in a function that its caller
calls inside the region. If an interrupt handler also uses the variable, the use
must be in a region with interrupts disabled, since an interrupt on this
processor could otherwise run in the middle of it; the message says so and names
the handler. The body of a handler marked //pu:interrupt, which the processor
enters through an interrupt gate, is such a region, up to a call of x86.Sti or a
call that may switch tasks (rt.Yield, rt.Preempt). The body of a //pu:interrupt
trap handler is not, so it opens a region of its own.

A use through a pointer from &v (section 20.9) is a use of the variable too:
when a handler uses the variable, it must be in a region with interrupts
disabled, except a lone store of a whole variable of at most a machine word.

Go has no per-processor variables. A Plutonium kernel keeps its processor's
state in them, and an ordinary variable would be shared by every processor and
need a lock. The compiler checks the uses instead, so that the most common use
(a pointer to the running thread) stays safe code.

For example:

    //pu:percpu
    var ticks uint64

    func count() {
        ticks++
    }

Written correctly:

    //pu:percpu
    var ticks uint64

    func count() {
        rt.DisablePreemption()
        defer rt.EnablePreemption()
        ticks++
    }

See "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9.

E2023: PerCPUPointer

&v for a //pu:percpu variable v points into the copy of the processor that runs
the code that takes it. The pointer is good only while the task cannot move to
another processor, so it is bounded by the region it was taken in (preemption
disabled, interrupts disabled, or an rt.Lock held), that one time the region
ran. It may be kept only in local variables declared inside the region's code,
so not in a variable that lives across the passes of a loop or of a goto, nor in
one that a function literal captures, and a parameter that the function sets to
&v is such a variable when the function can be called again with the result, as
a recursive function can. It may not be used after that region ends, in another
region (even one opened at the same place), or by another task, and may not be
sent on a channel, passed to a task, stored in a package variable or in memory,
or returned. The message says which of these the program does.

Use the variable by name inside the region, or copy its value out.

Go has no per-processor variables. A pointer to this processor's copy, used
after the task has moved, reaches the copy of another processor, which that
processor's code may be using at the same time.

For example:

    rt.DisablePreemption()
    p := &ticks
    rt.EnablePreemption()
    *p = 0

Written correctly:

    rt.DisablePreemption()
    ticks = 0
    rt.EnablePreemption()

See "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9.

E2101: BadPackedBacking

The backing type of a packed type says how its bits are stored. It must be an
unsigned or signed integer type (uint32), an array of bytes ([8]byte), or
[]byte. It can also be left out.

For example:

    type R packed float64 { a bits[uint, 4] }

Written correctly:

    type R packed uint64 { a bits[uint, 4] }

See "packed types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.14.

E2102: PackedTooLarge

The fields of a packed type are laid out one after another, from bit 0. Their
widths add up to more bits than the backing type holds.

Use a larger backing type, make fields narrower, or leave the backing type out
so the size follows the fields.

For example:

    type R packed uint8 {
        a bits[uint, 6]
        b bits[uint, 4]
    }

Written correctly:

    type R packed uint16 {
        a bits[uint, 6]
        b bits[uint, 4]
        _ bits[uint, 6]
    }

See "packed types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.14.

E2103: BadBitField

A bit field is written bits[T, N]: N bits read as type T. T must be an integer
type at least N bits wide, an enum, or bool with N equal to 1.

For example:

    mode bits[uint8, 12]

Written correctly:

    mode bits[uint16, 12]

See "packed types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.14.

E2104: BadPackedField

A field of a packed type must have a fixed bit layout: an integer, bool, float,
packed type, or an array of those. Strings, slices, pointers and structs have no
fixed layout in bits.

See "packed types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.14.

E2105: BadPackedLiteral

A packed literal must name each field it sets, as field: value. The fields of a
packed type have no order a literal could follow, since reserved bits sit
between them.

For example:

    r := ControlReg{5, true}

Written correctly:

    r := ControlReg{mode: 5, enable: true}

See "packed types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.14.

E2106: WrongWrapperArgs

volatile takes one type argument: volatile[T]. aligned and ring take a type and
a constant: aligned[T, N] and ring[T, N].

See "volatile[T]" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.15.

E2107: NotPowerOfTwo

The alignment of aligned[T, N] and the size of ring[T, N] must be a power of
two: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 and so on. Hardware alignment is always a power of two, and
a ring of that size finds a slot with a mask instead of a division.

For example:

    var buf ring[byte, 100]

Written correctly:

    var buf ring[byte, 128]

See "aligned[T, N]" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.16.

E2108: BadVolatileElem

volatile[T] makes every read and write of T exactly one load or store. That is
only possible for a value the processor moves in one instruction: an integer, a
bool, a pointer, or a packed type backed by an integer.

For example:

    var status volatile[[4]uint32]

Written correctly:

    var status [4]volatile[uint32]

See "volatile[T]" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.15.

E2109: BadWrapperSize

The second argument of aligned[T, N] and ring[T, N] must be a positive integer
constant, since the layout is fixed when compiling.

See "aligned[T, N]" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.16.

E2110: RingLiteralNotEmpty

A ring literal cannot list elements. Write an empty literal, or use the zero
value, and add elements with Push.

For example:

    r := ring[int, 4]{1, 2}

Written correctly:

    var r ring[int, 4]
    r.Push(1)
    r.Push(2)

See "ring[T, N]" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.17.

E2201: MisplacedDefault

Default parameter values are allowed only on the parameters of a declared
function or method. Receivers and results cannot have them, and neither can
function types, function literals or interface methods, because a call through a
function value or an interface does not know which function it calls, so it
could not fill in the defaults.

For example:

    type Grower func(n int, factor int = 2) int

See "Default parameter values" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.13.

E2202: VariadicDefault

A variadic function (one whose last parameter is ...T) cannot have default
values. An argument after the variadic ones could not be told apart from them,
and a default before them would have to be given whenever any variadic argument
is.

Variadic functions in the standard library allocate from alloc.Default instead;
alloc.Use replaces it for a region of code.

For example:

    func Logf(format string, args ...any, w io.Writer = os.Stderr)

See "Default parameter values" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.13.

E2203: MissingDefault

A call may leave out only trailing parameters. So once a parameter has a default
value, every parameter after it needs one too.

For example:

    func Grow(n int, factor int = 2, limit int) int

Written correctly:

    func Grow(n int, limit int, factor int = 2) int

See "Default parameter values" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.13.

E2204: SharedDefault

A parameter with a default value needs a name and type of its own. In a, b int =
1 it is unclear whether the default belongs to a, to b, or to both.

For example:

    func f(a, b int = 1)

Written correctly:

    func f(a int = 1, b int = 1)

See "Default parameter values" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.13.

E2205: DefaultRefersToParam

A default value is evaluated by the caller, in the scope where the function is
declared, before the function runs. The function's parameters do not exist yet
at that point, so a default cannot use them.

Default to a sentinel value instead, and compute the real value in the body.

For example:

    func Fill(buf []byte, n int = len(buf))

Written correctly:

    func Fill(buf []byte, n int = -1) {
        if n < 0 {
            n = len(buf)
        }
        ...
    }

See "Default parameter values" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.13.

E2301: MisplacedEnum

An enum type can only be the type of a type definition, type E enum { ... }. It
cannot be written inline in a variable or field, or be an alias, because its
members are reached through its name (E.Member).

For example:

    var c enum { Red; Green }

Written correctly:

    type Color enum { Red; Green }

    var c Color

See "Enums" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.12.

E2302: GenericEnum

An enum type cannot have type parameters. Its members are constants of one fixed
integer type.

See "Enums" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.12.

E2303: BadEnumBase

The type after the enum keyword is the integer type that holds the enum's
values. It must be an integer type such as uint8 or int32. Leave it out to let
the compiler pick the smallest one that fits.

For example:

    type Mode enum string { Read; Write }

Written correctly:

    type Mode enum { Read; Write }

See "Enums" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.12.

E2304: EmptyEnum

An enum needs at least one member, and one member must have the value 0, since
that is the zero value of the type.

See "Enums" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.12.

E2305: BlankEnumMember

Every member of an enum needs a name. To skip a value, give the next member an
explicit value instead.

For example:

    type E enum { A; _; C }

Written correctly:

    type E enum { A; C = 2 }

See "Enums" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.12.

E2306: DuplicateEnumValue

Two members of an enum have the same value. Each value must belong to one
member, so that converting a number to the enum, and printing a value's name,
give one answer.

A member without a value is one more than the member before it, which is often
how two members end up equal.

For example:

    type E enum {
        A = 1
        B
        C = 2
    }

See "Enums" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.12.

E2307: EnumWithoutZero

A variable of an enum type starts as 0, like every Plutonium variable. So one
member must have the value 0, or a fresh variable would hold a value that is not
a member.

Add a member for the zero value, often named None, Unknown or Invalid.

For example:

    type Level enum {
        Low = 1
        High
    }

Written correctly:

    type Level enum {
        Unset
        Low
        High
    }

See "Enums" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.12.

E2308: EnumOverflow

A member's value does not fit in the enum's integer type. Pick a wider type
after the enum keyword, or leave the type out so the compiler picks one that
fits.

For example:

    type E enum uint8 { A; B = 300 }

See "Enums" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.12.

E2309: BadEnumValue

The value of an enum member must be a constant integer expression, such as 5, 1
<< 3 or another constant, since members are constants fixed when compiling.

For example:

    type E enum { A = 1.5 }

See "Enums" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.12.

E2310: EnumSelfReference

An enum's member values cannot use the enum being declared, because those values
are what decides the enum's integer type.

See "Enums" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.12.

E2311: UnexportedEnumMember

Enum members follow Go's rule for names: a member whose name starts with a
lower-case letter is visible only inside its own package.

See "Enums" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.12.

E2312: MissingEnumCase

A switch on an enum value that has no default case must list every member. That
way, adding a member to the enum shows every switch that needs to handle it.

Add a case for each missing member, or a default case if the remaining members
share one action.

Go does not check this. Plutonium does, because an enum is a closed set of
values, so the compiler can tell when one is forgotten.

For example:

    switch c {
    case Color.Red:
        ...
    }

Written correctly:

    switch c {
    case Color.Red:
        ...
    default:
        ...
    }

See "Switch statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.9.

E2313: BadEnumConversion

Only integers convert to an enum, and a constant converts only if it is the
value of a member. Anything else would make an enum value that is not a member.

For example:

    c := Color(7) // Color has no member with value 7

See "Enums" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.12.

E2314: UncheckedEnumConversion

A number that is not a constant may not be the value of any member, so
converting it to an enum needs a check. Use the comma-ok form: ok reports
whether the number is a member's value.

Go allows any integer in a named integer type. Plutonium enums are closed sets,
so a value outside the set is caught where it enters, not later in a switch.

Use the comma-ok form:

    c, ok := Color(n)
    if !ok {
        return errBadColor
    }

See "Enums" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.12.

E2315: UntypedEnumConstant

A number written in the source is not a member of an enum, so it cannot be used
where an enum value is expected. A number that is not a member's value would
make an enum value that is not a member, and one that is hides the member's
name.

Name the member, or convert the number: Color(1) is valid when 1 is a member's
value.

Go lets an untyped constant become any named integer type. Plutonium enums are
closed sets of named members, so a value is always one of them and the source
says which.

For example:

    var c Color = 1

Name the member:

    var c Color = Color.Green

See "Assignability" in the Plutonium spec, section 8.4.

E2316: EnumArithmetic

Enum values can be compared, but not added, multiplied, shifted or combined with
bitwise operators, since the result would usually not be a member. Convert to an
integer to do arithmetic.

Convert to an integer first:

    next := int(c) + 1

See "Enums" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.12.

E2317: RangeOverEnumValue

To loop over the members of an enum, range over the enum type itself, not over a
value of it.

Range over the type:

    for c := range Color {
        ...
    }

See "For statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.10.

E2318: NotEnumType

This operation needs an enum type, such as len(E) or for v := range E, and the
type given is not an enum.

See "Enums" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.12.

E2401: MisplacedUnion

A union type can only be the type of a type definition, type U union { ... }. It
cannot be written inline in a variable or field, or be an alias, because its
variants are reached through its name (U.V).

For example:

    type Alias = union { A int; B }

Written correctly:

    type Shape union {
        A int
        B
    }

See "Unions" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.13.

E2402: GenericPackedUnion

A packed union cannot have type parameters. Its layout is fixed bit by bit by
its backing type, its tag and its fields, so it cannot depend on a type
argument. An ordinary union can be generic.

See "Unions" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.13.

E2403: EmptyUnion

A union value holds one of its variants, so a union needs at least one. Variants
are written like struct fields: a name, and a payload type if the variant
carries data.

For example:

    type Option union {}

Written correctly:

    type Option union {
        None
        Some int
    }

See "Unions" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.13.

E2404: BadVariantDecl

Each variant of a union has a name of its own, which is not _, followed by its
payload type if it has one. Variants have no tags, and in a packed union each
variant has its own case clause.

For example:

    type Value union {
        *Node
        _ int
    }

Written correctly:

    type Value union {
        Ref  *Node
        Size int
    }

See "Unions" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.13.

E2405: TooManyVariants

A union numbers its variants with a tag of at most 16 bits, so it can have at
most 65536 variants. Group related variants into a variant whose payload is
another union or an enum.

See "Unions" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.13.

E2406: UnionOwnerPayload

This payload is an owner: an owned[T] box, or a type declared owner, such as a
file or a vec.Vec. A union that holds one owns the payload of the variant it
holds. It drops that payload when the union is dropped or given another variant,
and it moves instead of being copied, so that the payload has exactly one owner.
Plutonium never makes a type an owner silently, so the union must be declared
owner union.

If the union should only look at a value that something else owns, hold a
pointer in the payload instead.

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

For example:

    type Slot union {
        Empty
        Full owned[Buffer]
    }

Declare the union as an owner:

    type Slot owner union {
        Empty
        Full owned[Buffer]
    }

See "Unions" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.13.

E2407: BadUnionTag

A packed union has a backing type, which fixes its size, and exactly one named
field before its variants: the tag. The tag is a bool, integer or enum field
that occupies the same bits in every variant, and each variant lists the tag
values that select it.

For example:

    type PTE packed union uint64 {
        Swapped case false { slot bits[uint64, 63] }
    }

Written correctly:

    type PTE packed union uint64 {
        present bits[bool, 1]

        Swapped case false { slot bits[uint64, 63] }
        Mapped case true { frame bits[uint64, 40] }
    }

See "Unions" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.13.

E2408: BadVariantCase

The values after case in a packed union variant are the tag values that select
it. Each must be a constant of the tag's type that fits in the tag's bits, and
no value may select two variants, so that every bit pattern means at most one
variant.

For example:

    type Desc packed union uint8 {
        kind bits[uint8, 2]
        A case 1 {}
        B case 1, 4 {}
    }

Written correctly:

    type Desc packed union uint8 {
        kind bits[uint8, 2]
        A case 1 {}
        B case 2, 3 {}
    }

See "Unions" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.13.

E2409: VariantNotType

A variant of a union is not a type. U.V(x) and U.V{...} make a value of the
union type U holding the variant, and a type switch on a union gives you the
payload of the variant it holds, which has the payload's own type.

To keep a payload in a variable, give the variable the payload's type.

Go has no unions. Keeping variants out of the type system means a payload always
has its own type and methods, and generic unions need no special rules.

For example:

    var r DeviceState.Ready

Written correctly:

    var st DeviceState = DeviceState.Ready{irq: 11}

See "Unions" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.13.

E2410: UnknownVariant

The union has no variant with this name, or the type before the dot is not a
union, so it has no variants at all. The message lists the variants the union
has.

See "Unions" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.13.

E2411: UnexportedVariant

Union variants follow Go's rule for names: a variant whose name starts with a
lower-case letter is visible only inside its own package. A switch in another
package that cannot name it needs a default case.

See "Unions" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.13.

E2412: UnionLiteral

A union value holds one of its variants, so a literal must name the variant:
U.V{...} for a variant whose payload is a struct, array or packed type, or
U.V(payload) for any variant with a payload. A variant without a payload is the
value U.V itself. In a packed union, the variant sets the tag, so its literal
cannot.

For example:

    st := DeviceState{}

Written correctly:

    st := DeviceState.Off
    st = DeviceState.Probing{attempt: 1}

See "Unions" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.13.

E2413: NotAVariant

A type switch on a union value, and a type assertion on one, name the union's
own variants: U.V, or in a case of the switch the bare name V. A union value is
never nil, and a variant of another union, or a type, can never match.

A bare variant name is accepted only in the cases of a type switch, where the
switch says which union is meant.

For example:

    switch v := st.(type) {
    case int:
        ...
    }

Written correctly:

    switch v := st.(type) {
    case Probing:
        ...
    default:
        ...
    }

See "Unions" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.13.

E2414: MissingVariantCase

A type switch on a union value that has no default case must name every variant.
That way, adding a variant to the union shows every switch that needs to handle
it.

Add a case for each missing variant, or a default case if the remaining variants
share one action. A switch that names every variant needs no default to end a
function.

Go cannot check this for interfaces, whose set of types is open. A union's
variants are a closed set, so the compiler can tell when one is forgotten.

For example:

    switch s := st.(type) {
    case Off:
        start()
    case Probing:
        retry(s.attempt)
    }

Written correctly:

    switch s := st.(type) {
    case Off:
        start()
    case Probing:
        retry(s.attempt)
    case Ready, Failed:
    }

See "Switch statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.9.

E2415: PackedUnionNoDefault

A packed union's bits usually come from hardware or from converting a raw value,
so its tag may hold any value its bits allow, including values no variant names.
A type switch without a default case must therefore cover every value of the
tag; this one does not.

Add a default case that handles the other values.

For example:

    switch d.(type) {
    case Seg:
        ...
    case Int:
        ...
    }

Written correctly:

    switch d.(type) {
    case Seg:
        ...
    case Int:
        ...
    default:
        return errBadDescriptor
    }

See "Switch statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.9.

E2451: MissingIndexMethod

Library containers such as vec.Vec and hash.Map are indexed through methods.
x[k] calls x.Index(k), x[k] = v calls x.SetIndex(k, v), and v, ok := x[k] calls
x.IndexOk(k). The container's type does not have the method this use of x[k]
needs.

Add the method to the type, or call another of its methods directly.

Go builds maps and slices into the language. In Plutonium they are library types
that own their memory, so the index syntax is lent to them through methods.

To support v, ok := x[k], add:

    func (m *Table) IndexOk(k string) (int, bool) {
        ...
    }

See "Indexing library containers" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.11.

E2452: BadIndexMethod

The indexing methods have fixed forms, so that x[k] can be turned into a call:
Index(k K) V, SetIndex(k K, v V) and IndexOk(k K) (V, bool). They may not have
type parameters of their own or be variadic.

For example:

    func (v *Vec) Index(i int) (int, error)

Written correctly:

    func (v *Vec) Index(i int) int

See "Indexing library containers" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.11.

E2453: IndexMethodMismatch

The indexing methods of one type must agree: Index, SetIndex and IndexOk take
the same key type K, and use the same element type V. Otherwise x[k] would have
a different type depending on whether it is read or assigned, and x[k] += 1
could not read and write the same element.

For example:

    func (m *M) Index(k string) int
    func (m *M) SetIndex(k string, v int64)

Written correctly:

    func (m *M) Index(k string) int
    func (m *M) SetIndex(k string, v int)

See "Indexing library containers" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.11.

E2454: ContainerIndexAddress

x[k] on a container calls its Index method, which returns a copy of the element.
A copy has no address that would stay valid, and a pointer to the element itself
would be invalidated as soon as the container grows or rehashes and moves its
elements.

Call a method that returns the pointer explicitly, such as vec.Vec.Ptr or
hash.Map.Ptr. Its documentation says how long the pointer is valid.

&s[i] on a slice is safe because a slice never moves its elements. A container
that grows does, so Plutonium makes the pointer an explicit method call rather
than give it the same syntax as the safe case.

For example:

    p := &v[i]

Written correctly:

    p := v.Ptr(i)

See "Indexing library containers" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.11.

E2455: ContainerElemAssign

x[k] on a container is a copy that its Index method returns, so assigning to a
field or element of it would change only the copy. This is the same rule as Go's
for map elements.

Read the element, change it, and store it back with x[k] = v, or change it in
place through a pointer from a method such as Ptr.

For example:

    v[i].x = 1

Written correctly:

    v.Ptr(i).x = 1

See "Indexing library containers" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.11.

E2456: RangeOverContainer

A for range loop over a value that is not a string, integer, array, slice, map,
channel or function calls the value's All method and ranges over the iterator it
returns, an iter.Seq or iter.Seq2. This type has no All method.

Give the type an All method that returns an iterator, or range over one of its
other iterators.

Library containers such as vec.Vec and hash.Map are structs, not built-in types,
so the loop asks them for an iterator by name. vec.Vec and hash.Map have All, so
they range like Go's slices and maps.

Add an All method:

    func (t *T) All() iter.Seq2[string, int] {
        return func(yield func(string, int) bool) {
            // call yield for each key and value
        }
    }

See "For statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.10.

E2457: UnaddressableContainer

The container's indexing method has a pointer receiver, so x[k] needs the
address of x. Here x is a value that has no address, such as the result of a
function call.

Store the container in a variable first, or index through a pointer to it.

For example:

    v := load()["key"]

Store it in a variable first:

    m := load()
    v := m["key"]

See "Indexing library containers" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.11.

E2458: BadRangeMethod

A for range loop over a container calls its All method, which must take no
arguments and return an iterator: a function of the form func(yield func(K, V)
bool), such as iter.Seq[V] or iter.Seq2[K, V]. This type's All method has
another form.

For example:

    func (l *List) All() []int

    for x := range l { ... }

Written correctly:

    func (l *List) All() iter.Seq[int]

    for x := range l { ... }

See "For statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.10.

E2459: UnaddressableRange

A for range loop over a container calls its All method. That method has a
pointer receiver, so the loop needs the address of the container. Here the
container is a value that has no address, such as the result of a function call.

Store the container in a variable first, or range over a pointer to it.

For example:

    for k, v := range load() { ... }

Store it in a variable first:

    m := load()
    for k, v := range m {
        use(k, v)
    }

See "For statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.10.

E2461: OrNotFunctionCall

An or clause tests the error that a function returns as its last result. A
conversion, such as int64(x), and a call of a built-in function, such as len(s),
are written like calls, but they call no function and return no error, so there
is nothing for or to test.

Remove the or clause, or call a function that returns an error.

For example:

    n := int64(count()) or return

Written correctly:

    n := int64(count())

See "Or clauses" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.27.

E2462: OrNoError

The call before or must return a result of type error last, and the type must be
error itself. A function that returns no error, or no result at all, cannot fail
in a way that or can see. A type that implements error, a defined type based on
error, or a type parameter is not accepted either.

Remove the or clause, or change the function so that its last result has type
error.

With the type exactly error, the test is always the nil test of one interface
type, and err in the clause is the result itself, not a conversion of it.

For example:

    s := strconv.Itoa(5) or return

Written correctly:

    s := strconv.Itoa(5)

See "Or clauses" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.27.

E2463: OrClauseCompletes

The clause of an or statement runs when the call failed, and the code after the
statement uses the values the call did not produce. So the clause must leave: it
must be a return, break, continue or goto statement, a call of panic, or a block
that ends in one of these. An if statement in the block leaves only when it has
an else branch and both branches leave. A call of a function that never returns,
such as os.Exit or log.Fatal, does not count, since whether a function compiles
must not depend on the body of another.

End the clause with a return, break, continue, goto or panic.

The or clause is the short form of an if statement that tests an error and
leaves. Requiring the exit on the line keeps the code after the statement from
running with values that were never set.

For example:

    cfg := load(path) or log.Printf("loading: %v", err)

Written correctly:

    cfg := load(path) or {
        log.Printf("loading: %v", err)
        return err
    }

See "Or clauses" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.27.

E2464: OrNoErrorResult

An unbraced return with no operands in an or clause returns err as the
function's last result. This function has no result of type error last, so there
is nowhere to put err, and returning without it would drop the error.

Handle the error in a block clause, or return values that this function has.

The short form of return exists to pass an error up. In a function that cannot
return one, the program must say what to do with the error.

For example:

    func main() {
        load(path) or return
    }

Written correctly:

    func main() {
        load(path) or panic(err)
    }

See "Or clauses" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.27.

E2465: OrPostStmt

The post statement of a for statement runs at the end of each iteration. An or
statement cannot be the post statement, since a break or continue in its clause
would end or repeat the iteration that is ending.

Test the error at the end of the loop body instead.

For example:

    for i := 0; i < n; i = next(i) or break {
        ...
    }

Written correctly:

    for i := 0; i < n; {
        ...
        i = next(i) or break
    }

See "Or clauses" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.27.

E2500: NotYetSupported

The program is valid, but this compiler cannot compile this construct yet. It
stops with an error rather than produce wrong code.

Rewrite the code without the construct, or check whether a newer compiler
supports it.

See "No silent failures" in the Plutonium spec, section 21.2.

E2510: ExternWithBody

//pu:extern says that the function is written in C or assembly and linked in
under the given symbol. A function with that directive must be declared without
a body.

For example:

    //pu:extern strlen
    func strlen(s *byte) uint64 { return 0 }

Written correctly:

    //pu:extern strlen
    func strlen(s *byte) uint64

See "Assembly and C" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.4.

E2511: BadCSignature

Functions marked //pu:extern or //pu:export are called with the C calling
convention. Their parameters and results must be types C has: integers, floats,
bools, pointers, and structs and arrays of those. Strings, slices, interfaces
and func values have no C equivalent, and variadic functions are not supported.

Pass a pointer and a length instead of a string or slice.

Pass a pointer and a length:

    //pu:extern write
    func write(fd int32, buf *byte, n uint64) int64

    write(1, unsafe.SliceData(b), uint64(len(b)))

See "Assembly and C" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.4.

E2512: DuplicateExport

Two functions are exported under the same symbol name with //pu:export, or the
name is already used by another symbol. The linker needs each symbol name to be
unique.

See "Assembly and C" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.4.

E2513: GenericExport

A generic function is compiled once for each set of type arguments it is used
with, so there is no single function to export under one C symbol. Export a
non-generic wrapper that calls it with the types you need.

Export a wrapper:

    //pu:export sum_ints
    func sumInts(p *int, n int) int { return Sum[int](unsafe.Slice(p, n)) }

See "Assembly and C" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.4.

E2520: KernelFloat

In kernel mode, functions may not use floating point. When an interrupt or
system call enters the kernel, the processor does not save the floating-point
registers, so kernel code that used them would corrupt the state of the program
it interrupted.

Functions marked //pu:fpu may use floats. Whoever calls one must save the
floating-point state first, with x86.FPUBegin and x86.FPUEnd.

Copying a struct that contains float fields is allowed, since it uses only
integer registers.

Go has no kernel mode. Plutonium rejects floats here, rather than emulate them
in software, so that no hidden cost or library ends up in the kernel.

Mark the function, and save the state around calls:

    //pu:fpu
    func scale(x float64) float64 { return x * 1.5 }

    x86.FPUBegin(&area)
    y := scale(x)
    x86.FPUEnd(&area)

See "Floating point in kernel mode" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.7.

E2521: UnprintableArgument

print and println can write a boolean, an integer (an enum too), a
floating-point number, a string, or a type whose underlying type is one of
these. They can also write the address of a pointer or function. They cannot
write an owner type, even when its underlying type is an integer, and they
cannot write a struct, array, slice, map, or interface. They have no form for
these, so a program that prints them gets the same text from every compiler, and
they work in a program that has no standard library.

To print another value, use fmt. To print the number inside an owner, convert it
first: the conversion reads the value without moving it.

Convert an integer field, or use fmt:

    println(int32(fd))

    import "pluto/fmt"

    fmt.Println(x)

See "Bootstrapping" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.12.

E2530: DeferInLoop

A defer statement in a loop would add a deferred call on every iteration, and
each needs room in the function's stack frame until it returns. Plutonium gives
every deferred call a fixed slot in the frame, so a defer statement may run at
most once in each call of the function.

This defer is allowed in a loop only if every path from it leaves every loop
that contains it before it can reach the defer again. A path leaves by return,
by a call of panic or of a function that never returns, by goto to a label
outside the loop, by a break that ends the loop, or by a labeled continue of a
loop outside it. An unlabeled continue does not leave. The rule is the same for
a for statement, a range statement, the body of a loop over a function (for x :=
range seq) and a loop made by a backward goto.

If the deferred call should run at the end of each iteration, move the loop body
into a function of its own; its defer then runs at the end of each iteration.

Go keeps the deferred calls of a loop in a list on the heap. Plutonium never
allocates behind the program's back, and kernel stacks are small and fixed.

For example:

    for _, name := range names {
        f := open(name)
        defer f.Close()
    }

Move the body into a function:

    for _, name := range names {
        process(name)
    }

    func process(name string) {
        f := open(name)
        defer f.Close()
        ...
    }

See "Defer statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.19.

E2531: TooManyDefers

A function may contain at most 64 defer statements. Each has a fixed slot in the
stack frame. Split the function into smaller ones.

See "Defer statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.19.

E2532: DeferBuiltin

This builtin cannot be deferred directly. Wrap the call in a function literal
and defer that.

Defer a function literal:

    defer func() { n = len(s) }()

See "Defer statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.19.

E2534: DeferLitCapture

A function literal deferred in the body of a loop over a function runs when the
enclosing function returns, after the loop body has returned. The body's own
variables, including the loop variables, live in the body's frame, which is gone
by then, so the literal cannot capture them.

Pass the values the deferred call needs as arguments: arguments are evaluated
when the defer statement runs.

Go moves such variables to the heap. Plutonium never moves a variable to the
heap; a closure is valid only while the frame it captures from is.

For example:

    for x := range seq {
        if done(x) {
            defer func() { log(x) }()
            return
        }
    }

Pass the loop variable as an argument:

    for x := range seq {
        if done(x) {
            defer func(x int) { log(x) }(x)
            return
        }
    }

See "Range over functions" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.11.

E2542: ErrorsNewValue

errors.New must be called directly. Each place that calls it gets its own error
type, made when compiling, so taking errors.New as a value, or deferring it, has
no call site to make that type for.

In Go, errors.New allocates. Plutonium makes an error type per call site, with a
counter, so that it never allocates.

For example:

    newErr := errors.New

See "Sentinels and errors.New" in the Plutonium spec, section 16.3.

E2550: PerCPUInit

A per-CPU variable can only be initialized with a constant. Package
initialization runs before any processor has its per-CPU block, so there is
nowhere to store a value computed at run time. Set the variable at run time on
each processor instead.

See "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9.

E2551: PerCPUMode

//pu:percpu variables are reached through the GS segment register, which only a
kernel sets up. Build with --mode kernel, or remove the directive.

See "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9.

E2552: PerCPUSection

A per-CPU variable always lives in the .percpu section, so it cannot also be
placed in another section with //pu:section.

See "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9.

E2560: StackDirective

//pu:stack sets the size of the stack a hosted program runs on: the runtime
reserves that much address space before main starts. It belongs on func main in
package main, and takes a size in bytes, with an optional K, M or G suffix, from
64K to 64G. A freestanding program sets up its own stack, so the directive has
nothing to act on there.

Put it on main:

    //pu:stack 256M
    func main() { ... }

See "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3.

E2570: InterruptDecl

A //pu:interrupt function is entered by the processor, not called by Plutonium
code. So it must be a plain function with a body: not a method, not generic, and
not marked //pu:inline, since the processor needs one fixed address to jump to.

See "Interrupts" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.10.

E2571: InterruptSig

An interrupt handler receives what the processor pushes on the stack: a pointer
to the interrupt frame, and for some exceptions an error code. It returns
nothing, since it returns with iretq to the interrupted code.

Use one of these signatures:

    //pu:interrupt
    func timer(frame *x86.InterruptFrame)

    //pu:interrupt
    func pageFault(frame *x86.InterruptFrame, code uint64)

See "Interrupts" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.10.

E2572: GateArg

x86.InterruptGate and x86.TrapGate build the entry of the interrupt table that
enters a handler, and the compiler puts the handler's address in it. So the
first argument must be the name of a handler, known when the program is
compiled: a function marked //pu:interrupt for InterruptGate, and a function
marked //pu:interrupt trap for TrapGate. A func value, a deferred or started
call, a handler of the other form, a call through a variable and any other
expression are errors.

The form decides how the processor enters the handler. An interrupt gate clears
the interrupt flag, and a handler of the plain form relies on that: its body is
a region with interrupts disabled. A trap gate leaves the flag as it was, which
is what a handler that can be entered again needs.

Pass the name of a handler of the form the constructor takes:

    //pu:interrupt
    func timer(f *x86.InterruptFrame) { ... }

    idt[32] = x86.InterruptGate(timer, 0x08, 0)

See "pluto/arch/x86" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.8.

E2573: InterruptHandlerV

An interrupt handler saves every register and returns with iretq, so ordinary
code cannot call it, and it is not an ordinary func value. Use x86.InterruptGate
(or x86.TrapGate for a //pu:interrupt trap handler) to enter it in the interrupt
table.

Enter it in the table:

    idt[32] = x86.InterruptGate(timer, 0x08, 0)

See "Interrupts" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.10.

E2580: NoPanicHandler

A freestanding or kernel build has no operating system to report a crash to, so
the program itself must say what happens when it panics (print to a serial port
or screen, then halt). It does so with one function marked //pu:panic.

A kernel has no operating system to fall back on when it panics, so Plutonium
makes the program choose what happens at compile time, and checks the choice:
the runtime calls the handler directly, with nothing to register, so it works
from the first instruction.

For example:

    package main

    func main() {}

Written correctly:

    package main

    import (
        "pluto/arch/x86"
        "pluto/rt"
    )

    //pu:panic
    func onPanic(p *rt.Panic) {
        p.Print(rt.Console{})
        for {
            x86.Cli()
            x86.Hlt()
        }
    }

    func main() {}

See "Panics in freestanding programs" in the Plutonium spec, section 17.4.

E2581: DupPanicHandler

A program has exactly one panic handler, and another function is already marked
//pu:panic. Remove //pu:panic from all but one of them.

A kernel has no operating system to fall back on when it panics, so Plutonium
makes the program choose what happens at compile time, and checks the choice:
the runtime calls the handler directly, with nothing to register, so it works
from the first instruction.

See "Panics in freestanding programs" in the Plutonium spec, section 17.4.

E2582: PanicHandlerDecl

The runtime calls the panic handler, the function marked //pu:panic, with a
description of the panic, and never expects it to return. It is a plain function
with a body, not a method, not generic and not also an interrupt handler, that
takes a *rt.Panic and returns nothing.

A kernel has no operating system to fall back on when it panics, so Plutonium
makes the program choose what happens at compile time, and checks the choice:
the runtime calls the handler directly, with nothing to register, so it works
from the first instruction.

Declare it:

    //pu:panic
    func onPanic(p *rt.Panic) { ... }

See "Panics in freestanding programs" in the Plutonium spec, section 17.4.

E2583: PanicHandlerRet

A panic stops the program: it does not unwind, so there is nothing for the panic
handler to return to. Every path through it must end in an endless loop or in a
call of a function that never returns, such as os.Exit, or a function whose
every path ends the same way. A call through a func value or an interface, and
recursion, count as returning.

A kernel has no operating system to fall back on when it panics, so Plutonium
makes the program choose what happens at compile time, and checks the choice:
the runtime calls the handler directly, with nothing to register, so it works
from the first instruction.

For example:

    //pu:panic
    func onPanic(p *rt.Panic) {
        p.Print(rt.Console{})
    }

Written correctly:

    //pu:panic
    func onPanic(p *rt.Panic) {
        p.Print(rt.Console{})
        for {
            x86.Cli()
            x86.Hlt()
        }
    }

See "Panics in freestanding programs" in the Plutonium spec, section 17.4.

E2584: ContextWaits

Interrupt handlers run on top of whatever code the CPU was executing, and the
panic handler on top of whatever panicked. If either waits, for a lock or for
room in a channel, the code it interrupted can never resume to release it, and
the CPU stops. So nothing they can reach may call a function that may park (such
as sync.Mutex.Lock or time.Sleep), send or receive on a channel outside a select
with a default case, run a select without one, or allocate from an allocator
that may wait.

The check follows the calls the compiler adds as well as the ones written: the
drops at the end of a scope, over an assigned owner and for temporaries, the
release of a counted value whose count reaches zero, and the Free of an
allocator. A Drop that waits, or an allocator whose Free waits, is reported
where the value is dropped, even though no call is written there.

Calls through interfaces and func values count as calling every method or
function they could reach in the program.

A function without a body (assembly or C) is not looked into: it counts as not
waiting unless its declaration is marked //pu:blocking. A //pu:blocking mark on
a function with a body states a wait the compiler cannot see, such as a spin
until another processor sets a flag; it only adds this restriction.

Writing to an os.File, such as os.Stderr, waits for the poller when the file is
a full pipe or socket, and the check cannot tell which file a call writes to. In
a panic or interrupt handler, write to rt.Console{} instead of os.Stderr: it
writes to standard error with a system call that never waits.

A kernel that waits in an interrupt handler hangs, often only under load and far
from the cause, so Plutonium checks the whole program at compile time instead.

To hand work to normal code, use a non-blocking send:

    select { case work <- item: default: }

See "Interrupts" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.10.

E2585: ContextSpawns

Interrupt handlers run on top of whatever code the CPU was executing, and the
panic handler on top of whatever panicked. Starting a task allocates its stack
and takes the scheduler's locks, which the interrupted code may hold, so nothing
they can reach may use a task statement.

A kernel that waits in an interrupt handler hangs, often only under load and far
from the cause, so Plutonium checks the whole program at compile time instead.

To hand work to a task, wake one that waits for it, or send without waiting:

    select { case work <- item: default: }

See "Interrupts" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.10.

E2586: InitAllocates

In a freestanding build, which every kernel is, a package-level initializer or
an init function can reach an allocation: new, make, a map literal, an operation
that makes a string at run time, or a call that reaches one. A freestanding
program has no allocator while its packages are initialized; its entry function
installs one with alloc.SetDefault, after initialization has run. Initializers
that need no allocation, such as composite literals, &T{...}, slice literals,
conversions to an interface and errors.New, are placed in static storage and are
allowed.

Declare the variable without an initializer, and set it in the entry function
after alloc.SetDefault.

Go's runtime sets up its heap before any package is initialized. A freestanding
Plutonium program brings its own allocator, so there is none until its own code
installs it, and no console to report the failure on if an initializer tried.

For example:

    var cache = make(map[string]int)

Written correctly:

    var cache map[string]int

    func kernelMain(info *BootInfo) {
        alloc.SetDefault(newSlab(info))
        cache = make(map[string]int)
    }

See "Package initialization" in the Plutonium spec, section 15.2.

E2587: InitSpawns

Package initialization runs sequentially before main. Starting another task
there could let it read package variables before initialization finishes. A
package variable's initializer or an init function must not reach a task
statement or a library call that starts a task. The check follows calls,
including deferred calls, drops, interfaces and func values, through the
complete program.

Tasks may read package variables written only during initialization without a
lock. Finishing initialization before starting another task preserves that data
race rule.

Start the task from main or a function main calls:

    func main() {
        task worker()
    }

See "Package initialization" in the Plutonium spec, section 15.2.

E2588: NoGenMetadata

A checked freestanding or kernel build tags heap pointers, and the runtime keeps
their generations in metadata it allocates outside the heap. With no operating
system to map that memory, the program supplies it: a function exported as
pluto_gen_metadata, with the signature func(n uintptr) uintptr, that returns the
address of n bytes the runtime keeps for good, or 0 when it has none. The
runtime zeroes the memory and calls the function with its writer lock held, so
the function must not allocate from the heap. This build exports no such
function, or exports one with another signature.

The source is bound when the program is linked, like the panic handler, so a
kernel cannot start checked without one. The runtime asks for 64 KiB at a time,
and 512 KiB for each 4 GiB of address space the heap uses; metadata is about a
quarter of the tagged memory.

Export a metadata source, or build with --gencheck=false for a deliberate unchecked build:

    var metadata [4 << 20]byte
    var metadataUsed uintptr

    //pu:export pluto_gen_metadata
    func genMetadata(n uintptr) uintptr {
        if metadataUsed+n > uintptr(len(metadata)) {
            return 0
        }
        p := uintptr(unsafe.Pointer(&metadata)) + metadataUsed
        metadataUsed += n
        return p
    }

See "Generation checks" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.11.

E2590: GoCapturingLiteral

A function literal that uses variables of the function around it reaches them in
that function's stack frame. A task statement may start it after that function
has returned, when the frame is gone.

Start the literal with a sync.Scope instead: s.Task(func() {...}) waits for it
before s's block ends, so it may use the function's variables, a Mutex or a
WaitGroup among them. Or pass the values the literal needs as arguments: they
are copied when the task statement runs. Do not pass a Mutex, a WaitGroup or
another sync type this way, since the copy is a different one.

Go moves captured variables to the heap. Plutonium never allocates behind the
program's back, so captured variables stay in their frame (spec section 11.5).

For example:

    for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
        task func() {
            out <- i * i
        }()
    }

Pass the values as arguments:

    for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
        task func(i int, out chan int) {
            out <- i * i
        }(i, out)
    }

See "Task statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.12.

E2591: GoBuiltin

A task statement cannot start a built-in function such as println or close
directly. Wrap it in a function and start that.

See "Task statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.12.

E2592: BadScheduler

A program binds its scheduler with one package-level variable marked
//pu:scheduler, whose type, not an interface, implements rt.Scheduler: NewTask,
FreeTask, Spawn, Park, Wake and Yield. The runtime calls its methods directly.
This variable does not qualify, or the program marks more than one.

The scheduler is chosen when the program is linked, like the panic handler, so
the runtime calls it without an indirection and a program cannot run without
one.

See "The scheduler contract" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.5.

E2593: NoScheduler

This program starts tasks, uses channels or waits in pluto/sync, so it needs a
scheduler, and none is bound. Hosted builds bind one by default; a freestanding
program, such as a kernel, provides its own: a package-level variable marked
//pu:scheduler whose type implements rt.Scheduler.

Plutonium carries a scheduler only in programs that ask for one, and a kernel
supplies its own, so a freestanding program must say which.

Bind the kernel's scheduler:

    //pu:scheduler
    var scheduler kernelScheduler

See "The scheduler contract" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.5.

E2594: GoFrameClosure

A function literal that captures variables, and a method value such as c.handle,
keep their closure record (the code to call, with the captured variables or a
copy of the receiver) in the frame of the function that makes them. A func value
points to that record. A task statement copies its function and arguments, but
copying a func value copies only the pointer, so the task may call through it
after the frame is gone.

The compiler reports this where it sees the literal or the method value itself:
passed to a task statement, or held by a local variable assigned only once. A
func value that reaches the task start any other way (through a parameter, a
field, an element, or a variable assigned twice) is reported by the check of
views of the frame (E3003, E3004), since it may be such a closure.

Library functions that start a task with a func argument, such as
sync.WaitGroup.Task and time.AfterFunc, follow the same rule and the compiler
reports them the same way.

To let the task use the function's variables, start a function literal with a
sync.Scope declared in the function instead: s.Task(func() {...}) waits for it
before s's block ends.

Go moves captured variables and the receivers of method values to the heap.
Plutonium never allocates behind the program's back, so the record stays in its
frame (spec section 11.5).

For example:

    square := func() { out <- n * n }
    task square()

Pass the values the function needs, and start a function that takes them:

    task func(n int, out chan int) {
        out <- n * n
    }(n, out)

See "Task statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.12.

E2595: SchedulerTimers

Timers need two things from the scheduler: a clock, its Now method, which
returns monotonic nanoseconds, and a way to be called back, its ArmTimer method,
which asks for rt.RunTimers to be called once a deadline has passed. This
program uses timers (time.Sleep, a time.Timer or Ticker, rt.ParkUntil or
rt.Now), and the bound scheduler lacks one of them.

The scheduler contract keeps timers optional, so a kernel that never sleeps
needs no clock. A program that does sleep must get its clock from the kernel,
because only the kernel knows which timer hardware it drives.

Give the scheduler a clock, and run the timers from the timer interrupt:

    func (kernelSched) Now() int64 { return int64(ticks) * 1000000 }

    func (kernelSched) ArmTimer(deadline int64) { next = deadline }

    // in the timer interrupt handler:
    if kernelSched{}.Now() >= next {
        rt.RunTimers()
    }

See "The scheduler contract" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.5.

E2600: UnknownDirective

A comment that starts with //pu: and no space is a directive: an instruction to
the compiler. This one has a name the compiler does not know, so it would do
nothing. A misspelt directive that was ignored could silently leave out an
interrupt handler, a per-CPU variable or an inlining request, so the compiler
reports it.

The directives are: build, inline, noinline, nochecks, extern, export, section,
interrupt (and its form interrupt trap), noreturn, noescape, resultholds, panic,
blocking, scheduler, nosimd, fpu, percpu and stack.

A comment that is only a note should start with // and a space.

Go ignores unknown //go: comments, which makes a typo easy to miss. Plutonium
directives change what the program means, so a typo must not go unnoticed.

For example:

    //pu:interupt
    func timer(frame *x86.InterruptFrame) { ... }

Written correctly:

    //pu:interrupt
    func timer(frame *x86.InterruptFrame) { ... }

See "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3.

E2601: RetiredDirective

This directive was removed, so the line is a compile error instead of something
the compiler reads. Where the compiler now works the fact out, delete the line;
the program means the same without it. Where another directive replaced it,
change the name to the replacement.

Removed directives and what replaced them:

//pu:alloc: kernel code may allocate strings anywhere the allocator may be
called.

//pu:bytewise: the compiler proves that string slice bounds fall on rune
boundaries.

//pu:gofunc: the compiler finds which functions start tasks with their func
arguments.

//pu:schedhook and //pu:schedlate: the compiler knows the runtime's scheduler
hooks by name.

//pu:nobounds: replaced by //pu:nochecks, which removes the bounds checks and
the generation checks from the function's body.

//pu:noreturn on a function with a body: the compiler finds that a function
never returns when its body ends in a terminating statement. The directive is
still needed on a function without a body, written in assembly or C.

//pu:noescape on a function with a body: the compiler finds which pointer
arguments a function keeps by reading its body. The directive is still needed on
a function without a body, written in assembly or C.

Plutonium asks for a directive only for a fact the compiler cannot derive from
the program, so that using the language does not need knowledge of how the
compiler works.

For example:

    //pu:noreturn
    func halt() {
        for {
        }
    }

Written correctly:

    func halt() {
        for {
        }
    }

See "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3.

E2602: MisplacedDirective

A directive belongs in the doc comment of the declaration it applies to: the
lines of comment directly above it, with no blank line between. Each directive
applies to one kind of declaration, such as functions or package-level
variables, and //pu:build applies to a whole file and goes before the package
clause. In any other place the compiler would not see it and it would do
nothing.

Go's //go: directives are ignored when misplaced. Plutonium directives change
what the program means, so a directive that does nothing is an error.

For example:

    func f() {
        //pu:noinline
        g()
    }

Written correctly:

    //pu:noinline
    func g() { ... }

    func f() {
        g()
    }

See "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3.

E2603: DirectiveArgument

Some directives take an argument, such as the symbol name in //pu:extern, and
the rest take none. This directive was given text it does not take, or lacks the
argument it needs, so the compiler would not understand it. To explain why a
directive is there, write an ordinary comment on a line of its own.

Go's //go: directives ignore text they do not expect. Plutonium directives
change what the program means, so they are read exactly.

For example:

    //pu:noinline keeps the profile readable
    func slow() { ... }

Written correctly:

    // Out of line to keep the profile readable.
    //pu:noinline
    func slow() { ... }

See "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3.

E2604: GoDirective

A line comment that starts with //go: and no space is a Go directive. Plutonium
does not read //go: comments, so this one would sit in the file looking as if it
had an effect. Plutonium's directives start with //pu:, and the ones Go also has
are spelt the same after the prefix: //pu:build, //pu:noinline, and
//pu:noescape on a function without a body. Go's others, such as //go:generate,
//go:embed, //go:linkname and //go:nosplit, have no equivalent.

Write the //pu: directive, or delete the line.

Go ignores //go: comments it does not know, which makes a typo easy to miss.
Plutonium directives change what the program means, so a comment that looks like
one is never ignored.

For example:

    //go:noinline
    func slow() { ... }

Written correctly:

    //pu:noinline
    func slow() { ... }

See "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3.

E3001: ReturnFrameView

A function returns a view of its own frame: a pointer to one of its local
variables, a slice of a local array, a function literal that uses local
variables, or an interface holding a copy of a value in the frame. The frame is
reclaimed when the function returns, so the caller would get a view of memory
that no longer holds the value.

Return the value itself, which copies it, or moves it if it is an owner. To keep
it at a fixed address after the function returns, allocate it.

Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free it
later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the heap
behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the code.

For example:

    func load() *Config {
        var c Config
        c.timeout = 30
        return &c
    }

Written correctly:

    func load() Config {
        var c Config
        c.timeout = 30
        return c
    }

See "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3.

E3002: StoreFrameView

A view of the frame, such as a pointer to a local variable, is stored in memory
that lives longer than the frame: a package variable, memory reached through a
pointer the function was given, or memory that may be on the heap. When the
function returns, that memory would still point at the reclaimed frame.

Store the value instead of a pointer to it, or allocate the value so that it
lives as long as the place that keeps it.

Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free it
later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the heap
behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the code.

For example:

    var last *Entry

    func add(e Entry) {
        last = &e
    }

Written correctly:

    var last Entry

    func add(e Entry) {
        last = e
    }

See "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3.

E3003: PassFrameView

A view of the frame is passed to a function that keeps it after returning: the
function stores it in a package variable, in memory that outlives the call, or
passes it on to something that does. The compiler knows this from the function's
body, so no annotation is needed. Passing a view to a function that only uses it
during the call is always allowed. A func value that may be a closure of the
frame (a function literal that captures variables, or a method value) is a view
of the frame too: passing it to a function that starts a task with it, such as
sync.WaitGroup.Task, rt.Go or time.AfterFunc, is this error.

Give the function something that owns its memory, such as a buffer or a value
allocated for it.

Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free it
later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the heap
behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the code.

See "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3.

E3004: GoFrameView

A task statement starts a task that runs on its own, and may still be running
after the function that started it returns. A view of that function's frame
passed to it could then point at reclaimed memory. A func value that may be a
closure of the frame (a function literal that captures variables, or a method
value), reaching the statement through a variable, a field, an element or a
parameter, is such a view.

Pass the value itself, which copies it, or moves it if it is an owner. To run a
method of an owned or synced box on the new task, start it directly (task
p.Serve()): the task then takes the box itself, so there is no view of the
frame. To let the new task use the frame, start it with a sync.Scope declared in
the function (s.Task(func() {...})), which waits for it before its block ends.

Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free it
later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the heap
behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the code.

For example:

    task handle(&req)

Written correctly:

    task handle(req)

See "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3.

E3005: SendFrameView

A value sent on a channel may be received after the function that sent it
returns. A view of that function's frame sent this way could then point at
reclaimed memory.

Send the value itself, which copies it, or moves it if it is an owner.

Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free it
later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the heap
behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the code.

For example:

    ch <- &req

Written correctly:

    ch <- req

See "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3.

E3006: LoopFrameView

A pointer to a variable declared in a loop, or a function literal that uses one,
is kept after the iteration that made it: stored in a variable declared outside
the loop body, for example. Each iteration reuses the memory of the loop's
variables, so what was kept would see the values of later iterations instead of
its own.

Use the pointer or call the function literal within the iteration, store values
instead, or pass the value to the function literal as a parameter.

Go gives each iteration of a loop its own variables, so a function literal made
in one iteration keeps that iteration's values. Plutonium keeps variables in the
function's frame, which has room for one copy of each; giving every iteration
its own copy would need memory for each iteration, allocated behind the
program's back.

For example:

    var first func() int
    for i := 0; i < 3; i++ {
        if i == 0 {
            first = func() int { return i * 10 }
        }
    }

Written correctly:

    var first int
    for i := 0; i < 3; i++ {
        if i == 0 {
            first = i * 10
        }
    }

See "Loop iterations reuse their storage" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.4.

E3007: BadResultHolds

//pu:resultholds p says that a function keeps its parameter p only for as long
as its result lives: the result, an owner, holds p and gives it back when it is
dropped, as alloc.Use's Restore does. The compiler then treats the call as
returning p instead of keeping it. The directive must name a parameter, and the
function must store that parameter in its result.

For example:

    //pu:resultholds a
    func Use(a Allocator) Restore {
        SetDefault(a)
        return Restore{}
    }

Written correctly:

    //pu:resultholds a
    func Use(a Allocator) Restore {
        r := Restore{prev: Default(), cur: a}
        SetDefault(a)
        return r
    }

See "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3.

E3008: ResultHoldsOutsideStd

//pu:resultholds is a promise the compiler cannot check: that a function's
result gives a parameter back when it is dropped, as alloc.Use's Restore gives
back the allocator it installed. The standard library uses it for the few
functions that need it. A program's own code does not need it: the compiler
works out by itself what a function keeps.

Remove the directive. If the compiler then rejects a call because the function
keeps an argument, pass memory that lives long enough, such as an allocated
value or a package variable.

For example:

    //pu:resultholds a
    func Install(a *Arena) Undo {
        ...
    }

Written correctly:

    func Install(a *Arena) Undo {
        ...
    }

See "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3.

E3009: TempViewKept

A view of a temporary owner or counted box is kept: stored in a variable, in
memory or in an interface, or returned. The view may come from a field, an
element, a pointer conversion or a method. The temporary is dropped at the end
of the statement that makes it (or of the if or switch statement whose header
makes it), so what was kept would look at a value that has been dropped.

Pass the view to calls in the same statement, or keep the owner in a variable,
which lives until the end of its scope, and take the view through that variable.

The view does not own the memory it looks at. Nothing keeps the temporary after
the statement, so a stored view would outlive the value that keeps its memory
alive.

For example:

    data := readAll(f).Slice()
    use(data)

Written correctly:

    buf := readAll(f)
    data := buf.Slice()
    use(data)

See "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3.

E3010: EscapeLimit

The escape analysis of a function reached its memory limit (256 MB) or its work
limit (a billion steps) before it could finish: the function holds more distinct
pointers than the analysis can follow. The analysis never guesses past its
limits, since a guess could accept a program that keeps a pointer into a dead
frame, so it stops with this error instead.

Split the function into smaller functions.

The compiler proves that no pointer into a frame outlives it, without a garbage
collector to fall back on. It follows each function's pointers precisely
(section 18.3), and that work is bounded so that a build cannot run out of
memory.

Split the function into smaller functions, each handling part of the work:

    func handle(req *Request) {
        parseHeaders(req)
        route(req)
    }

See "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3.

E3100: ClosureReturned

A function literal that uses variables of the function around it, and a method
value such as c.handle, are closure records: the code to run, with where each
captured variable is or a copy of the receiver. A function that returns one
builds it, and the variables it captures, in a region of memory its caller
provides, so the closure lives as long as the caller's frame. That works when
the function itself makes the closure and returns it as a func, in an interface,
or in a struct or array result.

This function returns a closure record that cannot be built there: one made by a
function literal nested in it or by the body of a range-over-func loop, which
are functions of their own whose frames are gone when they return, or one its
results hold only through a pointer, for which its callers provide no region.

Go moves the captured variables and the closure to the heap. Plutonium never
allocates behind the program's back, so the record lives in a frame: the
maker's, or the region its caller provides.

For example:

    func newHandler() *Handler {
        h := new(Handler)
        count := 0
        h.serve = func() { count++ }
        return h
    }

Make the closure in the function that returns it, and return it by value:

    func counter() func() int {
        n := 0
        return func() int {
            n++
            return n
        }
    }

See "Closures and their regions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.5.

E3101: ClosureRegionLoop

A function that returns a closure builds it in a region its caller provides. For
a call in a loop that region is part of the caller's frame, and every
iteration's call uses the same region, so the closure one iteration gets is
overwritten by the next iteration's call. Keeping it past the iteration, in a
variable declared outside the loop, in a variable of the for statement (which
passes its value on to the next iteration) or in memory reached through a
pointer, would leave it changing underneath. A goto back to a label before the
call makes a loop too.

Passing the closure back to the same call, as in it = Filter(it, f), would make
each closure hold the one before it: a chain as long as the loop runs, which no
region of fixed size can hold.

Use the closure within the iteration, or declare the variable that holds it
inside the loop. To compose closures, compose a fixed number of them, or keep
what each step needs in a slice and return one closure that applies every step.

Go allocates each closure on the heap. Plutonium builds it in the caller's
frame, which has room for one closure per call site.

For example:

    all := vec.New[func() int]()
    for _, n := range sizes {
        all.Push(counterFrom(n))
    }

Use the closure within the iteration:

    for _, n := range sizes {
        next := counterFrom(n)
        println(next(), next())
    }

See "Closures and their regions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.5.

E3102: ClosureRegionCycle

A function that returns a closure builds it in a region its caller provides, and
that region holds the regions of the calls whose closures the function returns
in turn. When the function returns the closures of a call that can reach the
same function again, directly or through other functions, or through a func
value or an interface whose implementations do, its region would have to hold
itself. No fixed size fits, so the caller could not reserve it.

Build the closures in a loop instead of by recursion, or keep their state in a
value the caller owns, such as a struct with a method.

Go allocates each closure on the heap, however deep the recursion. Plutonium
builds closures in frames, whose sizes are fixed when compiling.

For example:

    func chain(n int) func() int {
        if n == 0 {
            return func() int { return 0 }
        }
        next := chain(n - 1)
        return func() int { return n + next() }
    }

See "Closures and their regions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.5.

E3201: DropNotOwner

A method named Drop runs automatically when a value is destroyed. That only
makes sense for a type whose values have exactly one owner: if the values were
copied as ordinary values are, every copy would run Drop and release the same
resource. A type with a Drop method must be declared owner, so that its values
move instead of being copied.

Drop also takes a pointer receiver, never a value receiver: a value receiver
would move the owner into the method, which would drop it again when it returns.
Write func (f *File) Drop().

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

For example:

    type File struct{ fd int32 }

    func (f *File) Drop() { syscall.Close(f.fd) }

Written correctly:

    type File owner struct{ fd int32 }

    func (f *File) Drop() { syscall.Close(f.fd) }

See "Owner types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.18.

E3202: MustBeOwner

An owner owns memory or another resource and releases it when it is dropped, so
it moves instead of being copied. A struct with an owner field, an array of
owners, an interface that embeds an owning interface, or a type defined from an
owner owns the same things, and copying it would leave two values that both
release them. Plutonium never makes a type an owner silently: its declaration
must say owner, written after the name, as in type Conn owner struct { ... }.
The keyword is on the declaration so that a change to the type of a field in
another package is reported at this one declaration, and not as a move error at
every place that copies a value.

If the type should only look at a value that something else owns, store a
pointer to it instead.

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

For example:

    type Conn struct {
        buf strings.Buf
    }

Written correctly:

    type Conn owner struct {
        buf strings.Buf
    }

See "Owner types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.18.

E3203: UnnamedOwnerStruct

A struct that contains an owner owns what that owner owns, so it must be an
owner too, and only a named type can be declared an owner. A struct type written
without a name, as the type of a variable or a field, cannot have owner fields.

Declare a named owner type for the struct.

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

For example:

    var conn struct {
        buf strings.Buf
    }

Written correctly:

    type Conn owner struct {
        buf strings.Buf
    }

    var conn Conn

See "Owner types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.18.

E3204: BadOwnerKind

owner may mark a struct, an array, an integer, a pointer or unsafe.Pointer, or
an interface type. Other kinds cannot own anything: a slice or an unsafe string
is a view of memory something else owns; numbers, booleans, enums, packed types
and strings are values that copy; owned, shared, synced and weak already have
kinds of their own.

To attach a resource to such a value, wrap both in a struct declared owner.

For example:

    type Bytes owner []byte

Written correctly:

    type Bytes owner struct {
        b []byte
    }

See "Owner types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.18.

E3205: OwnerAlias

owner can only appear in a type definition, type T owner .... An alias (type A =
T) is another name for an existing type and has that type's kind; it cannot add
a mark of its own.

For example:

    type A = owner Buf

Written correctly:

    type A = Buf // an owner if Buf is

See "Owner types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.18.

E3206: BadDropSignature

Drop runs automatically when an owner is destroyed. The compiler calls it with
no arguments and ignores any result, so it must have no parameters and no
results. If the method is not meant to run when a value is destroyed, give it
another name.

For example:

    func (f *File) Drop() error

Written correctly:

    func (f *File) Drop()
    func (f *File) Close() error

See "Owner types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.18.

E3207: OwnerTypeArg

Generic code copies the values of a type parameter freely unless its constraint
is marked owner. Copying an owner would leave several values that all free the
same memory, so an owner type can only be the type argument of a type parameter
declared [T owner any] (or owner with another constraint). Inside such code,
values of T move as owners do.

If the generic code moves each value at most once, mark its type parameter
owner. Otherwise pass pointers to the owners, or copies made with Clone.

The standard library marks the type parameters that admit owners, and the others
never do: the keys of hash.Set and hash.Map and the elements of vec.Fixed are
copied, so they cannot be owners. Keep owners in a vec.Vec, or as the values of
a hash.Map.

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

For example:

    func Fill[T any](s []T, v T)

    Fill(bufs, strings.Buf{})

Written correctly:

    func Fill[T owner any](s []T, v T)

See "Owner types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.18.

E3208: BadTakeArg

take(p) moves the value p points to out of its place and leaves the zero value
there. It needs the place's address, so that it can write the zero value back: a
pointer such as &x.field, &s[i] or a pointer variable.

For example:

    left := take(n.left)

Written correctly:

    left := take(&n.left)

See "Moving out of a place" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.10.

E3209: BadUnsafeDropArg

unsafe.Drop(p) destroys the value p points to, as the end of its owner's life
would: it runs the value's Drop method and then drops its fields. It needs a
pointer to the value. It belongs to the unchecked layer that owner types are
written with, such as alloc.Free: the compiler does not stop the value from
being used or dropped again afterwards.

For example:

    unsafe.Drop(v)

Written correctly:

    unsafe.Drop(&v)

See "Package unsafe" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.1.

E3210: BoxOfInterface

owned[T], shared[T] and synced[T] hold one object of type T on the heap. An
interface already refers to the value it holds, so a box around an interface
would be a second, slower way to own a value behind an interface, with two
levels of pointers.

To own whatever is converted into an interface, declare an owning interface. To
box the value itself, use its own type.

For example:

    var r owned[io.Reader]

Written correctly:

    type Source owner interface {
        Read(p []byte) (int, error)
    }

    var r Source

See "owned, shared, synced and weak" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.19.

E3211: BadWeakElem

A weak reference refers to an object that counted references keep alive, without
keeping it alive itself, so that structures with back pointers can be freed.
Only shared[T] and synced[T] count their references, so weak takes one of them:
weak[shared[Node]].

For example:

    parent weak[Node]

Written correctly:

    parent weak[shared[Node]]

See "owned, shared, synced and weak" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.19.

E3212: OwnerConversion

Converting a plain value into an owner type makes a new owner of whatever the
value refers to: a file descriptor, a page frame. If code outside the owner's
package could do that, two owners of the same resource could exist, and both
would release it. So only the package that declares the owner type may convert
into it, as only that package keeps its invariants.

The same limit holds for every other way to make an owner from something its
package did not make: converting another owner type into it (allowed also in the
package of a type defined from it), a pointer conversion from or to a pointer to
it (which needs the conversion both ways), a conversion that makes an owner
union, and a composite literal of an owner struct with an unexported field.

Converting an owner into a plain value only reads it and is allowed anywhere. A
pointer view cannot become a box at all: make one with new or the alloc package.

For example:

    fd := sys.Fd(3) // sys declares type Fd owner int32

Written correctly:

    fd, err := sys.Open("/dev/null")

See "Conversions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.24.

E3213: MapOfOwner

A map copies its keys and elements as Go's map does: v := m[k] and range give
copies of them. An owner cannot be copied, because two copies would both release
what it owns, so neither the key type nor the element type of a map may be an
owner.

Store a counted reference to the value instead: a synced[T] or shared[T] copies
by counting, and the last copy releases the value.

Go's garbage collector frees what a map's values refer to. Plutonium frees
memory when its owner is dropped, so a map, whose elements are copied freely,
can hold values and counted references but not owners.

For example:

    files := map[string]os.File{}

Written correctly:

    files := map[string]synced[os.File]{}

See "Map types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.10.

E3214: OwnerOperator

An operator other than a comparison is applied to a value of an owner type. An
owner may be compared, but no other operator applies to it, whatever its
underlying type: f + 1 for type Fd owner int32 would be a new Fd made by
arithmetic, a second owner of whatever the first refers to.

Convert the owner to its underlying type, which reads its value and moves
nothing, and apply the operator to the result.

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

For example:

    func next(f *Fd) int32 {
        return *f + 1
    }

Written correctly:

    func next(f *Fd) int32 {
        return int32(*f) + 1
    }

See "Operators" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.18.

E3301: UseAfterMove

A value of an owner type owns memory or another resource. Assigning it, passing
it by value, returning it, sending it or storing it moves it instead of copying
it, so that what it owns has exactly one owner. The variable it left holds
nothing until it is given a new value, and using it before then is an error.

If both places need a value, copy it explicitly with Clone. If the later code
only needs to look, give it a pointer (&m) instead of the value.

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

For example:

    n := m
    m.Set("a", 1)

Written correctly:

    n := m.Clone()
    m.Set("a", 1)

See "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5.

E3302: UseMaybeMoved

A variable of an owner type is moved on some paths to this point and not on
others, for example in one branch of an if, or in an earlier iteration of a
loop. On the paths where it moved it holds nothing, so it cannot be used here.

Use it before the move, give it a new value on the path that moves it, or
declare it inside the loop so that each iteration has its own.

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

For example:

    for _, line := range lines {
        flush(buf)
    }

Written correctly:

    for _, line := range lines {
        flush(buf)
        buf = strings.Buf{}
    }

See "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5.

E3303: MoveOutOfPlace

An owner can be moved only out of a whole variable. A field, an element, a value
reached through a pointer or a package variable would still believe it holds the
owner, and the owner would be released twice.

Use take(&place) to move the value out and leave the zero value behind, a
pointer (&place) to look at it without moving it, or Clone to copy it.

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

For example:

    left := n.left

Written correctly:

    left := take(&n.left)

See "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5.

E3304: RangeCopiesOwner

A range loop with a value variable copies each element into it. Copying an owner
would leave two values that both release what it owns, so the elements of a
slice or array of owners cannot be ranged over by value.

Range over the indexes and use a pointer to each element.

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

For example:

    for _, b := range bufs {
        use(&b)
    }

Written correctly:

    for i := range bufs {
        b := &bufs[i]
        use(b)
    }

See "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5.

E3305: MoveCaptured

A function literal uses the variables of the function around it where they live.
It may run several times, or never, so if it moved one of them away the function
around it could not tell whether the variable still holds a value that it must
drop.

Pass the owner to the literal as an argument instead, or move it before or after
the literal runs.

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

For example:

    do := func() { send(b) }

Written correctly:

    do := func(b strings.Buf) { send(b) }
    do(b)

See "Function literals" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.4.

E3306: CopyOwners

copy, and append of a whole slice (append(s, t...)), duplicate the elements they
copy. Duplicating owners would leave two values that both release what each
owns.

Move the elements one at a time with take, or copy each one explicitly with
Clone.

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

For example:

    copy(dst, src)

Written correctly:

    for i := range src {
        dst[i] = take(&src[i])
    }

See "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5.

E3307: DropThroughView

Drop releases what an owner holds, and runs by itself when the owner is dropped.
Calling it explicitly ends an owner's life early, and is allowed only on a
variable that holds the owner, whether or not its type declares a Drop method,
which cannot be used afterwards. Through a pointer, a field, a method value or
an ordinary interface, the value belongs to something else, which would drop it
a second time.

Call Drop on the owner itself, or leave the zero value behind with take.

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

For example:

    func closeIt(f *File) { f.Drop() }

Written correctly:

    func closeIt(f File) { f.Drop() } // takes the File and ends its life

See "Drops" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.6.

E3308: OwnerFromLender

Converting an owner to an ordinary interface such as any lends it: the interface
looks at the owner, which still belongs to the variable that was converted.
Taking an owner type out of such an interface would make a second owner.

Assert to an interface type and call the value's methods, or declare an owning
interface (type R owner interface {...}) and move the owner into it.

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

For example:

    b := x.(strings.Buf)

Written correctly:

    if s, ok := x.(fmt.Stringer); ok {
        use(s.String())
    }

See "Type assertions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.13.

E3309: IndexOwner

x[k] on a library container calls its Index method, which returns a copy of the
element. When the elements are owners, the copy would be a second owner of what
the element owns.

Use the container's methods instead: one that returns a pointer to the element
to look at it (Ptr), or one that removes or replaces it to move it out.

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

For example:

    b := bufs[0]

Written correctly:

    b := bufs.Ptr(0)

See "Indexing library containers" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.11.

E3310: VariadicOwners

Variadic arguments are passed in an array that the function sees as a slice. A
slice is a view and never owns its elements, so owners passed that way would be
released by nobody.

Pass the owners one at a time, or in an owner of its own such as a vec.Vec.

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

See "Passing arguments to ... parameters" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.15.

E3311: DeferInnerOwner

Deferred calls run when the function returns. An owner declared in an inner
block is dropped when that block ends, before the deferred call would run, so
the call would use what the owner had released.

Declare the owner at the top level of the function, or do the work at the end of
the block instead of deferring it.

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

See "Defer statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.19.

E3312: SliceLitOwners

A slice literal keeps its elements in an array that the slice only looks at.
Owners stored there would be released by nobody.

Use an array literal, which is an owner of its elements, or a vec.Vec.

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

For example:

    bufs := []strings.Buf{a, b}

Written correctly:

    bufs := [2]strings.Buf{a, b}

See "Composite literals" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.3.

E3313: StaleView

A view taken from an owner held in a local variable (&c, a slice of a local
array of owners, a method value c.M, c.Slice()) looks at memory that the
variable owns. It may be used only while the variable keeps the value the view
was taken from. Once the variable is moved, dropped or given a new value, the
view looks at memory that was released, or at a value that belongs to something
else.

The same holds for text in a local arena (a string made while the arena is the
default allocator, or by a Builder started with the arena as its allocator):
converting it to an interface in the function that owns the arena puts a copy in
the function's frame, which holds a reference to the text until the function
returns, after the arena was dropped. Write the text's bytes instead of
converting the string.

Use the view before the owner is moved, or move the owner only after the last
use of the view. If both are needed, move a copy made with Clone.

Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has no
garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a view
must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.

For example:

    p := &c
    consume(c)
    p.Write(data)

Written correctly:

    p := &c
    p.Write(data)
    consume(c)

See "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3.

E3314: LostView

A view of an owner held in a local variable may be used only while the variable
keeps the value the view was taken from. The compiler follows views through
local variables, their fields, and the calls that store them into other
arguments. A view stored anywhere else (through a pointer, in a variable that a
function literal uses or whose address is kept, or by a call the compiler cannot
see into) cannot be followed, so the owner may not be moved, dropped or given a
new value while that view could still be used.

Keep the view in a local variable or a field of one, or move the owner only
where no such view of it exists.

Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has no
garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a view
must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.

For example:

    pp := &h
    *pp = &c
    consume(c)

Written correctly:

    h = &c
    use(h)
    consume(c)

See "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3.

E3315: ConsumingMethodValue

A method whose receiver is an owner type taken by value consumes its receiver:
calling it moves the receiver into the method. A method value c.M holds its
receiver, so for such a method it would own c, and every call of it would
consume c again. A consuming method therefore has no method value.

Call the method directly, or use the method expression T.M, which takes the
receiver as its first argument when it is called.

Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to
when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner
frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one.

For example:

    done := c.Close

Written correctly:

    done := Conn.Close
    done(c)

See "Method values" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.9.

E3401: ScopeOutlivesVar

A task started by s.Task holds a pointer, slice or function literal that looks
at a variable whose storage is reused before s waits for the task. A variable
declared in a loop body is new in each iteration in Go, but in Plutonium every
iteration reuses the same memory, so a later iteration would overwrite what the
task is still looking at.

The task takes its own copy of each such variable that it uses directly, so use
the variable itself in the function literal rather than a view of it. Or wait
for the task before the iteration ends, with s.Wait() at the end of the loop
body.

Go keeps the variables a goroutine uses alive with its garbage collector,
however long the goroutine runs. Plutonium has none: a variable lives in its
function's frame until its block ends. A sync.Scope waits for the tasks it
started before its block ends, so they may use the function's variables, and the
compiler checks that nothing they use ends or moves first.

For example:

    for _, line := range lines {
        var buf [64]byte
        v := buf[:copy(buf[:], line)]
        s.Task(func() { send(v) })
    }

Written correctly:

    for _, line := range lines {
        var buf [64]byte
        n := copy(buf[:], line)
        s.Task(func() { send(buf[:n]) })
    }

See "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4.

E3402: ScopeRace

A task started by s.Task uses a variable, or memory a variable points to, at the
same time as another task or the function that started it, and one of them
writes it. Two tasks writing one place at once can lose an update, and a value
larger than a word can be read half written: a data race.

Share such a place only under one lock that every use holds (the compiler infers
which), use a type from pluto/sync/atomic, give each task its own part of a
slice or array (x[i] with i the loop variable), or read the result after
s.Wait(), which waits for the tasks.

Two slices of one type may overlap. Tasks may use both at once only as out[i]
and in[i], with the same loop variable, and the program then checks, as each
task starts, that the slices start at the same element or do not overlap.

Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run under,
and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks run in
parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data races, and the
compiler checks it from the program itself, with no annotations.

For example:

    var total int
    for _, n := range counts {
        s.Task(func() { total += n })
    }

Written correctly:

    var mu sync.Mutex
    var total int
    for _, n := range counts {
        s.Task(func() {
            mu.Lock()
            total += n
            mu.Unlock()
        })
    }

See "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4.

E3403: ScopeNotPartitioned

Several tasks started by s.Task write elements of one slice or array, and the
compiler cannot see that no two of them write the same element. It can see that
for x[i], where i is the variable of the loop that starts the tasks (each task
takes its own i), for a window x[i*k:(i+1)*k] of such an i, and for x[:m] in one
task and x[m:] in another.

Index by the loop variable, give each task a window of the slice and index
within it, or send the results on a channel and store them in the function that
started the tasks.

Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run under,
and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks run in
parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data races, and the
compiler checks it from the program itself, with no annotations.

For example:

    for i := range in {
        s.Task(func() { out[idx[i]] = f(in[i]) })
    }

Written correctly:

    for i := range in {
        s.Task(func() { out[i] = f(in[i]) })
    }

See "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4.

E3404: ScopeMoveInUse

A variable is moved or dropped while tasks started by s.Task may still use it.
Moving an owner leaves the variable empty, and dropping it frees what it owns,
so the tasks would use freed memory.

Wait for the tasks first, with s.Wait(), then move or drop the variable.

Go keeps the variables a goroutine uses alive with its garbage collector,
however long the goroutine runs. Plutonium has none: a variable lives in its
function's frame until its block ends. A sync.Scope waits for the tasks it
started before its block ends, so they may use the function's variables, and the
compiler checks that nothing they use ends or moves first.

For example:

    s.Task(func() { fill(&buf) })
    consume(buf)

Written correctly:

    s.Task(func() { fill(&buf) })
    s.Wait()
    consume(buf)

See "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4.

E3405: ScopeMoved

A sync.Scope that has started tasks is moved: assigned, passed or returned. The
tasks it started find it at its address, to tell it when they return, so after a
move nothing would wait for them in the right place.

Keep the Scope in the variable that started the tasks. To wait for the tasks
elsewhere, pass the work instead, or call s.Wait() before the Scope goes.

Go keeps the variables a goroutine uses alive with its garbage collector,
however long the goroutine runs. Plutonium has none: a variable lives in its
function's frame until its block ends. A sync.Scope waits for the tasks it
started before its block ends, so they may use the function's variables, and the
compiler checks that nothing they use ends or moves first.

For example:

    var s sync.Scope
    s.Task(work)
    return s

Written correctly:

    var s sync.Scope
    s.Task(work)
    s.Wait()

See "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4.

E3406: ScopeConcurrentCall

A function literal is passed to a function that calls it from several tasks at
once, and the literal writes a variable it captures. The calls would write that
variable at the same time: a data race.

Use an atomic type for the variable, or lock a mutex around the write in the
literal.

Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run under,
and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks run in
parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data races, and the
compiler checks it from the program itself, with no annotations.

For example:

    count := 0
    parallel.Map(names, lens, func(s string) int { count++; return len(s) })

Written correctly:

    var count atomic.Int64
    parallel.Map(names, lens, func(s string) int { count.Add(1); return len(s) })

See "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4.

E3407: ScopeTakenWrite

A task started by s.Task changes a variable that it takes: the variable's block
(a loop body, or the loop's own variables) may end before s waits for the task,
so the task gets its own copy of the variable when s.Task runs, as each
iteration of a Go loop gets its own variables. The function that started the
task uses the variable afterwards, and would not see the change.

Declare the variable inside the function literal if only the task needs it. If
the function that started the task needs the result, declare the variable before
the loop and give each task its own part of it (results[i] = ...), or wait for
the task with s.Wait() in the same block, before the variable is used again.

Go keeps the variables a goroutine uses alive with its garbage collector,
however long the goroutine runs. Plutonium has none: a variable lives in its
function's frame until its block ends. A sync.Scope waits for the tasks it
started before its block ends, so they may use the function's variables, and the
compiler checks that nothing they use ends or moves first.

For example:

    for _, it := range items {
        n := 0
        s.Task(func() { n = work(it) })
        s.Wait()
        if ok {
            continue
        }
        println(n)
    }

Written correctly:

    for i, it := range items {
        s.Task(func() { results[i] = work(it) })
    }
    s.Wait()

See "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4.

E3501: RaceSendView

A pointer or slice is handed to another task, as an argument of a task statement
or a value sent on a channel, but what it points to may change while both tasks
can use it. One task could then read memory the other is writing, which is a
data race.

A view may cross to another task only if what it points to cannot change while
both may use it: a package variable that is not written once tasks run, a struct
type that no code changes through a pointer after making it, memory of a
pluto/sync type, or the object of a synced[T]. Pass a copy of the value, move an
owner such as a vec.Vec, or share the data through a synced[T] whose fields a
lock guards.

Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run under,
and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks run in
parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data races, and the
compiler checks it from the program itself, with no annotations.

For example:

    func bump(c *Counter, done chan bool) {
        c.n++
        done <- true
    }

    c := new(Counter)
    task bump(c, done)

Written correctly:

    func bump(n int, done chan int) {
        done <- n + 1
    }

    task bump(c.n, done)
    c.n = <-done

See "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3.

E3502: RaceNoLock

Data that several tasks use is written without a lock that guards it: a package
variable written by code more than one task runs, or a field of a synced[T]
object. Two tasks could write it, or one write it while another reads it, at the
same time. An interrupt handler is one of these parties: it may run at any point
where interrupts are enabled, on any processor, and on several processors at
once, so data it writes needs a guard even when no task uses it. The message
names the handler.

The compiler infers which lock guards the data from the locks held where it is
written: a sync.Mutex (or the write lock of a sync.RWMutex) that every write
holds, or an rt.Lock for data a handler uses. Hold the same lock at every write
and every read, or use an atomic type from pluto/sync/atomic. A volatile[T] or a
region with interrupts disabled excludes only code on the same processor, so it
does not guard data a handler shares with other processors; it is enough only
for a per-CPU variable (section 20.9).

Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run under,
and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks run in
parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data races, and the
compiler checks it from the program itself, with no annotations.

For example:

    var hits int

    func handle() {
        hits++
    }

    // started with task handle() from several places

Written correctly:

    var (
        mu   sync.Mutex
        hits int
    )

    func handle() {
        mu.Lock()
        hits++
        mu.Unlock()
    }

See "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3.

E3503: RaceUnguarded

Data that several tasks use is read without the lock that guards it. Every write
to it holds the same lock, so the compiler infers that the lock guards it, and
every read must hold it too: a read without it may run while a write is half
done. An interrupt handler counts as one of the tasks, and the message names it.

Hold the lock (a sync.RWMutex's RLock is enough, or an rt.Lock for data a
handler uses) around the read. If the writes use atomic operations, read it with
an atomic operation too.

Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run under,
and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks run in
parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data races, and the
compiler checks it from the program itself, with no annotations.

For example:

    wg.Wait()
    println(counter)

Written correctly:

    wg.Wait()
    mu.Lock()
    n := counter
    mu.Unlock()
    println(n)

See "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3.

E4001: NotMainPackage

pluto build and pluto run build a program, and a program starts in package main,
at func main. The directory given holds a different package, which can only be
imported by a program or tested with pluto test.

See "Package clause" in the Plutonium spec, section 14.2.

E4002: NotPackageDir

pluto test takes a directory holding a package, or the import path of a standard
library package such as pluto/strings. This path is neither.

E4003: TestDirName

A package outside any module is imported by its tests under the name of its
directory. That name is reserved here, since main and pluto/... already mean
something else. Rename the directory, or add a pluto.mod file to make the
package part of a module.

Declare a module in the directory above:

    module example.com/project

E4004: TestMainPackage

pluto test builds a program of its own that calls the tests, so it cannot test
package main, which already is a program. Move the code under test into a
package of its own, and import it from main.

E4005: StdPackageMissing

Import paths that start with pluto/ name packages of the standard library, and
this one does not exist. Check the spelling. Go's packages have Plutonium
versions under pluto/, such as pluto/fmt for fmt, but not every Go package has
one.

See "Import declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 14.3.

E4006: PackageNotFound

The import path is inside this program's module, as declared by its pluto.mod
file, but no directory exists at the matching place. An import path is the
module path followed by the package's directory, relative to the directory
holding pluto.mod.

E4007: ImportNoModule

Without a pluto.mod file, a program is a single package that can import only the
standard library (paths under pluto/). To split a program into packages, add a
pluto.mod file in its top directory that names the module, and import packages
by the module path followed by their directory.

Create pluto.mod in the top directory:

    module example.com/project

    // and import a package in ./util as:
    import "example.com/project/util"

E4008: ImportOutside

A program can import the standard library (paths under pluto/) and packages of
its own module (paths under the module path in pluto.mod). This import path is
neither, and Plutonium has no package downloads.

E4009: ImportCycle

Packages import each other in a circle. A package must be compiled after every
package it imports, so a circle has no order that works. Move the code both
packages need into a third package that both import.

See "Import declarations" in the Plutonium spec, section 14.3.

E4010: NoSourceFiles

The package's directory has no .p files for this build. Either it has none at
all, or every file is left out by a //pu:build line or a file name suffix such
as _linux.p for the target and mode being built.

See "Source file organization" in the Plutonium spec, section 14.1.

E4011: ExternalTestPkg

Test files in Plutonium belong to the package they test. A _test.p file in a
package named x_test, which Go allows, is not supported. Declare the test in the
package itself.

E4012: MixedPackages

All the .p files in one directory must belong to the same package, so they must
all start with the same package clause.

See "Package clause" in the Plutonium spec, section 14.2.

E4013: BadConstraint

A //pu:build line is a boolean expression over build tags, using && (and), ||
(or), ! (not) and parentheses, such as //pu:build amd64 && !kernel. This one
does not parse.

See "Build constraints" in the Plutonium spec, section 14.4.

E4014: BadModFile

The first line of a pluto.mod file must be the word module followed by the
module path.

Write the first line as:

    module example.com/project

E4015: ReservedModule

Import paths under pluto/ belong to the standard library, so a module cannot use
one as its path. Choose another, such as a domain you control followed by the
project name.

E4016: NoMainFunc

A program starts by calling func main in package main, and this package does not
declare one.

Add a main function:

    func main() {
    }

See "Program execution" in the Plutonium spec, section 15.4.

E4017: ReadPackage

The compiler could not read the package's directory. Check that the path exists
and that you have permission to read it.

E4018: BadTestSignature

pluto test runs the functions in _test.p files whose names start with Test or
Benchmark. Such a function must take exactly one parameter, a *testing.T for a
test or a *testing.B for a benchmark, and return nothing.

Declare it like this:

    func TestParse(t *testing.T) {
        ...
    }

E5001: UndefinedSymbol

The linker joins the compiled program with its assembly, C and object files, and
found a symbol that is used but defined nowhere. This happens when a function is
declared without a body but no .s, .c or .o file in its package defines it, or
when //pu:extern names a C function that is not linked in.

See "Assembly and C" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.4.

E5002: RelocOverflow

The compiled code reaches every symbol with a 32-bit offset, so code and data
must all lie within 2 GiB of each other. In hosted builds they are near address
0; in kernel mode they are in the top 2 GiB of the address space. The linker
placed a symbol outside that range, usually because a linker script puts a
section somewhere else.

In kernel mode, link the image at or above 0xFFFFFFFF80000000, as the default
kernel linker script does, and keep the .percpu section at address 0.

See "Kernel mode" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.6.

E5003: MultipleDefs

Two objects define the same symbol. This happens when a .s, .c or .o file
defines a function that Plutonium code also defines, when a //pu:export name is
also defined in C, or when two C files define the same global.

See "Assembly and C" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.4.

E5004: AssemblerError

The GNU assembler rejected a .s file of the program. Its messages follow, with
the file's own name and line. Assembly files use GNU assembler syntax; Plutonium
functions are referred to by their import path and name, in quotes, such as
"pluto/syscall.Syscall".

See "Assembly and C" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.4.

E5005: CCompilerError

The C compiler rejected a .c file of the program. Its messages follow. C files
are compiled freestanding: there is no C library, so headers such as stdio.h are
not available.

See "Assembly and C" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.4.

E5006: LinkFailed

The linker failed. Its messages follow. If a linker script was given with
--ldscript, check that it exists and places every section the program has.

See "Build modes and targets" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.5.

E5007: PerCPUNotAtZero

Per-CPU variables are reached at their offset from the GS base, which only works
if the linker places the .percpu section at address 0, with the Plutonium object
first. The linker script given puts it elsewhere.

See "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9.

E5008: ToolNotFound

Plutonium uses the system's GNU assembler (as), linker (ld) and, for .c files, C
compiler (cc, or $CC). This one is not installed or not on the PATH. On most
Linux systems the binutils package provides as and ld.

E9001: InternalError

The compiler failed on a program that passed type checking. This is a bug in the
compiler, not in your program. Please report it with the smallest program that
shows it. Changing the code near the position shown may work around it.
